WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:11.200
Welcome, everyone to CS 336,
Language Models from Scratch.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.340
This is the teaching staff.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:13.020
I'm Percy.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.980
This is Tatsu, Marcel,
Herman and Steven,

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:19.180
and we're bringing you
the third edition of 336.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.640
So we'll just do a quick
round of introductions.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.720
So I'd like to say that
I've been doing language

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.440
models for 20 years,
but most of that time

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.200
was small language models.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.600
Actually, this is still small
in the grand scheme of things.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.640
I think when Tatsu and I
started teaching this class two

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.320
years ago, we
weren't really sure

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.260
what we were going to expect,
but we were very pleasantly

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.760
surprised that so
many people wanted

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.343
to learn how to build language
models from scratch, especially

00:00:45.343 --> 00:00:47.840
in these days when a
coding agent could probably

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.760
zero shot a language model.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.280
I'm really glad
to see all of you

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.680
here to actually want
to learn how they work.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:56.420
Yeah, thanks.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:58.128
I'm Tatsu, I'm one of
the co-instructors.

00:00:58.128 --> 00:01:00.480
I'll be talking
to you once we get

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.000
to architectures and scaling and
all these other very fun things.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:04.980
I'm really excited.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.680
This is the most fun class
I've taught in my time here.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.457
I think every year,
Percy makes fun of me

00:01:09.457 --> 00:01:12.040
because he says you have to redo
all your lectures because you

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.680
took on architectures, and
everything changes every time.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.220
But it's actually pretty
fun for me to do it.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.060
And it's the first time that
I've had that experience.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.602
So I'm looking forward to going
through this experience again

00:01:20.602 --> 00:01:23.400
with you all.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.960
Hello, I'm Marcel.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.760
I've seen this
class once before,

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.340
and I'm returning because it
was so much fun last time.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:30.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.120
It's a lot of work though.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.620
In my research, I do
architecture stuff,

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.080
I do higher order gradients,
and I do training.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.600
Yeah, looking forward to
working with you guys.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.600
Hello, everyone, I'm Herman.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.237
A year ago, I didn't really how
LLMs worked, so I think for me,

00:01:47.237 --> 00:01:49.320
tokens were the things you
collect in video games,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.320
and attention was the thing you
had, like the attention economy.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.300
Then after spending a lot of
time on this course last year,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.720
I'm now doing LLM research,
and I'm really excited to TA

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.360
this year.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.060
Hi, everyone.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.120
I'm Stephen.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:06.920
I am a first time
CA for this course

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.840
and I'm very excited about it.

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.520
I think it will be a lot of fun.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.760
Broadly, in my research, I
work on language models, theory

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.360
and some data efficiency stuff,
and I'm excited to meet you all.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:23.440
All right, so let's
go into things.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.960
So this is the third
time we're offering it.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.580
Last year, we decided to put
all our lectures on YouTube.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.520
So some of you have seen it.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.400
So what's new?

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.340
Well, what hasn't changed is
'from scratch' philosophy.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.120
We still believe strongly
that by building everything

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.440
from the ground up, you really
learn how everything works.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:48.880
Of course, we don't actually
build everything up from scratch

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.840
because that wouldn't
fit in the quarter.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.680
So over the last two years,
we've been refining our recipe

00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:58.930
and figuring out what are
the things that you build up

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:00.930
from scratch that are
the most high value?

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:05.250
And then finally, as Tatsu
alluded to, even in a year,

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.050
a lot has changed.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:11.170
I think this year we're going
to spend maybe a bit more time

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.570
on mixture of experts.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:16.950
And of course, agents are
very popular these days.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.410
So getting a handle
on long-context

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:23.930
and what is needed for that
are going to be important.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.870
So why did we make this course?

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.170
And I think the
problem two years ago

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.210
was that researchers were
becoming disconnected

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.150
from the underlying technology.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.890
It used to be the
case may 10 years ago,

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.970
all AI researchers would
just implement and train

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:43.410
their own models.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:46.770
And then even eight
years ago, people

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:50.330
would download pre-trained
models such as Bert

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:51.890
and fine tune them.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:55.610
And I think a lot of
today, you can get by

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.233
by simply prompting a model.

00:03:58.233 --> 00:04:00.650
And of course, there's nothing
wrong with prompting model.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.290
I think you can do
amazing things with it.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.970
I think moving up the
abstraction in general

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:09.650
is a great thing, but
abstractions are leaky,

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:12.750
and sometimes, I'm sure all
of you have prompted models,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.370
you run into situations
where something is--

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:18.490
you wanted to do something,
but it just can't do it

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:21.490
and there's no recourse.

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:23.810
And I would argue that if
you're really interested

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.990
in fundamental research, by
simply prompting a model,

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:31.410
you're vastly constraining
the set of options, the design

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:32.930
space you're looking at.

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:35.590
And by looking at
fundamental research,

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:38.650
you really need to tear
up the whole stack.

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.970
So I would argue that
full understanding of how

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.730
language models work
is really necessary

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:45.570
for fundamental research.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.930
And the way that we're going
to get an understanding

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.350
is by building.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.690
That's the philosophy
of the class.

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:54.210
But there's one
small problem, which

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:57.930
is that industrialization of
language models has happened.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.510
Frontier models are
really, really expensive.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.650
Even, this is three years ago.

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:07.230
GPT-4 was supposedly costing
$100 million to train,

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.590
and now costs probably are
on the order of $1 billion,

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:12.930
although that's speculative.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.490
And the number of GPUs that
all the big labs are building

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.730
is just immense.

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.570
Furthermore, there's no details
on how any of these models

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:26.330
are built. So even back in
2023, the GPT-4 paper explicitly

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.890
says that due to a competitive
landscape and safety

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.450
implications, we're not going
to share anything about how

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.650
the models are being built.

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:35.930
So these frontier
models are in some sense

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:38.090
out of the reach for us.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.770
Now, we could build
small language models,

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:43.050
and we will build
language models.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:46.410
But I think it's important to
remember that these might not

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.990
be representative of the
actual frontier models.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:54.570
And I'll give you two examples
why this might be the case.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.450
So here's one.

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:59.210
This is from actually
quite a long time ago.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.490
I think back in 2021.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:03.850
If we're going to
spend more time on flop

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:06.470
counting and looking at where
the compute is being spent.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:09.250
But if you look at small
scales, the fraction

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:14.650
of flops spent in the
MLP layers is around 44%.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:20.010
And if you scale up to
175B, then it goes to 80%.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:23.530
So what you optimize and
what matters at large scale

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:25.470
is going to be different
from small scale.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.220
So if you did a bunch
of small scale stuff

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:31.650
here on the attention, you might
not experience the same benefits

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:33.650
at large scale.

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:40.130
The second example is that we
know of emergence of behavior

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:41.050
with scale.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:45.050
So small models, this is, again,
for a while ago, but even back

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:50.340
then, if you have a
0 shot or a few shot

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.500
learning of various
tasks, it basically,

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.980
seemed like nothing
was working, and only

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.980
when you reach a critical
scale do you suddenly

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.660
see a lot of improvement.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:02.760
So again, if you're
working on small scale,

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.340
you might not see certain
types of phenomenon

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:09.180
compared to if you were
working at full scale.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:14.240
OK, so that might be a little
bit disheartening, but fear not,

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.160
we're going to learn
something in this class.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.300
And the question is, what can we
learn that actually transfers?

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.340
And I think it's important
to break this down

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:24.380
into three types of knowledge.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:27.740
First, there's the
mechanics of how

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:31.500
things work, what a transformer
is, how model parallelism works.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.900
There's mindset,
which is, how do you

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:37.637
go about approaching
building a language model?

00:07:37.637 --> 00:07:40.220
We're going to talk about how
you want to squeeze the most out

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:42.940
of your hardware, taking
scaling seriously.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:44.540
And then finally, intuitions.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:46.860
Which data modeling
decisions are going

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.380
to yield good performance?

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.800
So now, in this
class, I think we

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.300
can do a pretty
good job of teaching

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:57.173
the mechanics, which is how
things work and the mindset.

00:07:57.173 --> 00:07:59.340
And we're going to really
emphasize that you profile

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.580
and benchmark everything and
try to optimize for efficiency.

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:06.460
These things do transfer
to a larger scale.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:09.540
Now, the intuitions about
what modeling decisions

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:14.460
and what data decisions do
work might not necessarily

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:16.618
transfer across scales.

00:08:16.618 --> 00:08:18.660
For that, you actually
have to go somewhere where

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.340
you can do things at scale.

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:25.060
So on the note of
intuitions, it is

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:30.180
worth remarking that some
design decisions are just

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:35.039
not justifiable, and just purely
come from experimentation.

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:38.240
Whereas mechanics, you
can, by construction,

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.620
see how the parallelism
and how kernels are going

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:44.140
to speed things up and so on.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:47.452
But for intuitions about
what modeling changes work,

00:08:47.452 --> 00:08:49.160
I think you just have
to run experiments.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.460
There's this famous
Noam Shazeer paper

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.940
that introduces the
SwiGLU activation, which

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:55.560
we're going to look about.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.780
And in the conclusion
section, he very honestly

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:03.380
has this final sentence which
says, we offer no explanation.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:06.000
We attribute their success, of
these architectures, all else,

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.540
to divine benevolence.

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:11.660
So that, in some sense,
is something that you just

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:15.180
have to gain from experience.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.980
Final note about the bitter
lesson, which I think

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:21.980
has been circulating and
people talk about it.

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:25.760
I think there's a common
misconception here,

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:27.002
what it means.

00:09:27.002 --> 00:09:28.460
I think the wrong
interpretation is

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.360
that scale is all that matters.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.140
Algorithms don't matter.

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:33.920
But that's not correct.

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.580
The right interpretation is
that algorithms that scale are

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:39.580
what that-- or what matters.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:42.300
And you can think
about very simply

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:46.540
that the accuracy of your model
is basically your efficiency

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:48.840
times the resources.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:50.300
So efficiency is
output over input,

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:53.020
and resources is the input.

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:56.940
And efficiency is
actually, in some sense,

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.320
way more important at
larger scale, right?

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:01.320
If you're doing a
small scale experiment,

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:05.300
if your run takes twice
as long, maybe you just

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.920
wait twice as long and
then you come back later.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.020
But if you're doing
things at scale,

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:13.880
that could be hundreds
of millions of dollars.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:15.640
And you definitely
don't want to do that.

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:19.220
Even like a 5% improvement
might be a big deal.

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:21.280
So in fact, efficiency
is actually really,

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:22.040
really critical.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.940
And I hope to bake
that into your mindset

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:28.620
as one of the consequences
of this class.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:32.540
And empirically, if you look
at-- there's this paper from

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:36.460
OpenAI in 2020 that showed
there's a 44x algorithmic

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:40.950
efficiency on ImageNet
between 2012 and 2019.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:45.670
And it's surely the case that
hardware did get a lot better,

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:49.450
but that also comes with
algorithmic improvements.

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:51.850
And of course, when you
multiply them together,

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:59.390
that's when you see a huge bump
in efficiency and accuracy.

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:02.610
So the framing
with all that said

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:07.590
is, what is the best model one
can build with a certain data

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:09.650
and compute budget?

00:11:09.650 --> 00:11:11.150
For pre-training,
it's mostly, we're

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:12.608
going to talk about
compute budget,

00:11:12.608 --> 00:11:15.390
because we're going to assume
that we have a lot more data

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:17.350
than we have compute.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:20.630
But if you're in a setting
where you're data limited

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:25.330
or you have stashed away,
actually, tons of B200s,

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:29.190
then you might be data bound.

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:31.570
So in other words,
maximize efficiency.

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:33.590
And we're going to
see this theme come up

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:36.670
throughout the class.

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:38.050
OK.

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:42.110
So next, I want to spend a bit
of time talking about language

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:45.310
models and a bit of a history
and just contextualization

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:49.670
before we jump into the
more technical details.

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:52.870
So language models have
been around for a while.

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:55.910
Shannon, back in the '50s was
using language models to measure

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:58.190
the entropy of English.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:02.300
And for a long
time, N-gram models

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:04.550
were used, actually, in
machine translation and speech

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:07.590
recognition systems.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:09.450
And they weren't
the whole system,

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.367
but they were an important
part of making sure

00:12:11.367 --> 00:12:13.470
that you generated fluent text.

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:17.910
I would say that the lineage
of the Modern Language models

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:20.630
comes from neural architectures.

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:25.650
And so there is a bunch
of ideas, I think,

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:28.110
which are important
to this development.

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:32.390
So in the '90s, there was LSTMs.

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:36.430
Yoshua Bengio, actually, wrote
the first neural language model

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:38.690
in paperback in 2003.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.090
This is actually not an LSTM.

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:42.510
This was just a
feedforward network that

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:45.310
looked at the small context.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:49.290
And then there was a Seq2Seq
modeling, which boldly said,

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:53.910
we can actually compress a
whole sentence into a vector.

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:56.330
The atom optimizer
attention mechanism,

00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:58.550
which was developed for
machine translation.

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:00.550
The transformer architecture,
which built on top

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:01.925
of that, which
was also developed

00:13:01.925 --> 00:13:03.470
for machine translation.

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:08.070
And then scaling up to a mixture
of experts model parallelism.

00:13:08.070 --> 00:13:12.630
You see a lot of different
architecture and also systems

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:18.550
and optimizer ideas
developed in the 2010s.

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:20.710
And then by the
late 2010s, I think,

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:23.070
things were starting to
get really interesting.

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:27.250
So there was the ELMo and BERT.

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:31.370
These are language models that
were trained on lots of text,

00:13:31.370 --> 00:13:32.790
and then you could
fine tune them

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:35.010
on some downstream tasks
like question answering,

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:37.910
and it would show a huge
improvement on that.

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:41.310
So the model there was, take
one of these models and then

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:42.750
fine tune.

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:46.530
And then Google had a
paper that was really,

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:51.750
I think, foreshadowing this
view of prompt in, response out.

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:54.230
So it was really,
I think, OpenAI

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:57.350
that really opened
up the floodgates

00:13:57.350 --> 00:13:59.370
here by embracing scaling.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:03.790
So they had GPT paper back
in, I think, 2018 or so.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:11.310
They scaled it up to GPT-2, and
then they really figured out

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:13.670
or embrace the idea
of scaling laws,

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:16.390
which we're going to
talk about in a second,

00:14:16.390 --> 00:14:20.510
which enable them
to train GPT-3,

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:24.830
which was a much more
massive, almost more than 10x,

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:26.972
the largest model at the time.

00:14:26.972 --> 00:14:28.430
And it could show
emergent behavior

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:30.470
like in-context learning.

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:34.570
At that point, Google
was saying, OK,

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:36.757
we need to do something too.

00:14:36.757 --> 00:14:38.090
So they trained a massive model.

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:40.430
It turned out to
be undertrained.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:42.710
And it turned out that their
DeepMind, which was not

00:14:42.710 --> 00:14:44.250
integrated with
Google at the time,

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:49.430
had figured out optimal-compute,
optimal scaling laws.

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:52.010
So all of this was happening.

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:56.370
And after GPT-3 came out,
I think for many folks,

00:14:56.370 --> 00:14:58.790
this was kind of a wake up call.

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:01.510
And at that time, there were
a lot of early attempts to,

00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:03.870
let's try to replicate this.

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:05.470
So there was a
grassroots organization

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:08.690
called Eleuther that created
some open data sets and models.

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:11.670
They weren't very large because
they didn't have much compute.

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:17.430
Meta first LLM, you could
tell that it's a replication

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:21.190
because it was like
175 billion parameters,

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:23.950
but it was not a
very good model.

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:27.390
They ran into a lot
of hardware issues.

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:30.980
And then there was another,
Hugging Face BigScience project.

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:35.200
So these models were, I
would say, not very strong.

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:38.520
Then in the last three years, I
think, the open model ecosystem

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.960
has changed quite
a bit, with Meta

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:43.960
kind of leading the way with
the Llama series of models.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:46.200
Llama, Llama 2, Llama 3.

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:47.880
Mistral got in the game.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:53.640
And then a whole set
of Chinese models.

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:55.060
I think I'm missing some.

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:59.185
Like, ByteDance has something,
and I think Tencent probably

00:15:59.185 --> 00:16:00.060
has some other stuff.

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:01.980
So it's hard to keep
track of everything,

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:04.560
but everyone's heard
of DeepSeek and Qwen.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:07.820
So I think I got the main ones.

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:10.800
But I think what's interesting
and exciting about these is now

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:14.540
we have open weight models that
are approaching closed models.

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:16.860
So depending on who you
ask and how you benchmark,

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.080
they might be a little
bit behind or comparable.

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:22.080
But they are definitely
very, very credible models

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:25.520
that are being widely
used in industry.

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:28.860
Now there's another
line of work,

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:33.040
which is going beyond just
releasing open weight models.

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:38.320
AI2, NVIDIA and the Marin
project, which I work on,

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:42.680
we try to provide not just
the weights, but the paper

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:44.400
and the code and
the data so that we

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.840
can understand how
these models are

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:51.360
built in a more thorough way.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:55.360
Why do I emphasize this
open ecosystem so much?

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:57.700
Mostly because this course
would not be possible,

00:16:57.700 --> 00:17:01.600
I think, without these
models by the fact

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:04.800
that there are still
many papers that

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:08.760
are being published about
how these big MOEs and RL

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:12.880
systems are working enables
us to at least glimpse

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:15.400
into how these frontier
models are being built,

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:17.440
and trying to
triangulate the pieces.

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:19.780
Now, I think, of course,
a lot of the details,

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:22.898
even with these Qwen
papers, I think they're

00:17:22.898 --> 00:17:23.940
missing a lot of details.

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:25.020
So can't reproduce them.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:28.760
Notably, the data mixture is
something that we don't, but I

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:32.440
think it's much
better than nothing.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:32.940
OK.

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:37.400
So in the last decade,
I think, the idea

00:17:37.400 --> 00:17:40.320
of what a language model
has changed, right?

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:43.960
It used to be something
that you fine-tuned,

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:45.760
and then it was
something you prompt.

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:48.960
And now in the ChatGPT era,
it was something you talked to

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:52.600
and that you can have
a conversation with.

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:55.300
And now-- OK, I guess
I don't have internet.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:56.000
[CHUCKLING]

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:58.280
That's fine.

00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:00.895
And now, we're in
the era of agents.

00:18:00.895 --> 00:18:02.520
If you click on this
link, it basically

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:04.840
shows you a giant agent trace.

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:09.840
And I'm still-- it's
mind boggling how strong

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:10.940
some of these models are.

00:18:10.940 --> 00:18:12.800
You give it like a
page of text and it

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:16.680
does some really complicated
agentic coding task.

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:20.320
So what we demand of our
language models today

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:23.800
is probably beyond the
imagination of any one back

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:25.640
10 years ago.

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:28.900
That said, I think the
fundamentals, I think,

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:30.600
haven't changed that much.

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:33.840
Largely, we still built
on GPUs and kernels.

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:37.000
We still optimize using
gradient or stochastic

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:38.500
gradient-like approaches.

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:41.180
We still have the
transformer in attention,

00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:43.900
And we'll talk a little bit
more about architectures,

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:46.880
but it hasn't changed that much.

00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:48.840
I think the specs are different.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:51.700
Now we demand greater
context lengths,

00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:55.343
which means that inference
efficiency matters even more.

00:18:55.343 --> 00:18:56.760
So the good news
for us is that we

00:18:56.760 --> 00:18:59.440
didn't have to completely
change this class.

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:02.820
Only Tatsu's section on the
latest Chinese architectures.

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:03.320
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:07.560
But the fundamentals,
I think, are here

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.960
to stay, at least for now.

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:13.160
OK, maybe I'll pause
there in case there's

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:15.660
any questions or thoughts.

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:26.370
OK.

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:28.850
So let's go on.

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:30.450
So a brief interlude.

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:32.570
So what is this program?

00:19:32.570 --> 00:19:34.790
So this is what I call
a executable lecture.

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:38.230
If you think it looks
like a Python program,

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:41.530
it is because it is
actually a Python program,

00:19:41.530 --> 00:19:45.650
but it has been rendered
for your viewing pleasure.

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:50.130
So when I step through it as
actually executing the lecture.

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:54.170
And it makes us possible
to basically step

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:56.250
through code, which
will be, hopefully,

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:57.583
interesting and important later.

00:19:57.583 --> 00:19:59.542
And you can also see the
hierarchical structure

00:19:59.542 --> 00:20:00.230
of this lecture.

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:02.022
For example, we're done
with this function.

00:20:02.022 --> 00:20:03.770
Now, we go back to main.

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:07.690
OK, so let's talk about
the course logistics,

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:10.290
and the syllabus,
which I think is going

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:14.290
to take a good chunk of time.

00:20:14.290 --> 00:20:18.090
So all the information
is online at this website

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:21.410
or cs336.stanford.edu.

00:20:21.410 --> 00:20:23.530
This is a 5-unit class.

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:26.830
I think this, probably, class
has a certain reputation,

00:20:26.830 --> 00:20:30.970
so I don't need to belabor the
point too much, but this is--

00:20:30.970 --> 00:20:32.430
we have five assignments.

00:20:32.430 --> 00:20:34.090
They are pretty intense.

00:20:34.090 --> 00:20:38.650
Even the first assignment
according to this one.

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:42.310
Review was actually equivalent
to the 5 assignments.

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:44.170
First, CS 224n.

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:50.050
I've been told, also, this
is exaggerated, but better

00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:54.930
to, I guess, be conservative
in your estimates.

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:58.350
So why should you-- so why
should you take this course OK,

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:00.730
so first you have
an obsessive need me

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:02.590
to understand how things work.

00:21:02.590 --> 00:21:04.590
That should be your
primary objective.

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:08.410
I think just pure curiosity
on how language models work.

00:21:08.410 --> 00:21:10.730
And then in doing
the class, you'll

00:21:10.730 --> 00:21:14.370
develop a much stronger
research, engineering muscles

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:16.890
and have the confidence
to go into a new setting

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:22.410
and be equipped to deal with
whatever situation comes up.

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:25.110
So I think when I
started at Stanford,

00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:27.530
I created this class
called Statistical Learning

00:21:27.530 --> 00:21:30.930
Theory, which was, basically,
teaching the theoretical side

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:32.130
of machine learning.

00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:34.790
And that was nice because
it equipped people.

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:37.210
So when you read a paper, you
can understand all the math.

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:39.170
And now, since the
field has shifted

00:21:39.170 --> 00:21:41.610
a lot more towards this more
systems and empirical side

00:21:41.610 --> 00:21:46.490
of things, this is the kind
of analogous class, which

00:21:46.490 --> 00:21:49.730
gives people enough depth
that they feel that everything

00:21:49.730 --> 00:21:52.850
else seems kind of easy.

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:55.102
So why should you
not take this course?

00:21:55.102 --> 00:21:56.810
This is important
because there's reasons

00:21:56.810 --> 00:21:58.930
you shouldn't take this course.

00:21:58.930 --> 00:22:02.602
First, you actually want to get
some research done this quarter.

00:22:02.602 --> 00:22:04.310
You should probably
talk to your advisor.

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:05.630
They should know you're
taking this course.

00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:07.330
Otherwise, there'll
be-- probably,

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:10.450
there might be some surprises.

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:12.410
You're interested in
learning the hottest

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:14.690
new techniques in AI.

00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:18.710
I think there are many other
great courses, seminar courses,

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:20.550
topics courses that
are good for that.

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:22.250
We don't do many of the things.

00:22:22.250 --> 00:22:23.590
We don't do multimodality.

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:27.550
We don't talk about
agents in any depth.

00:22:27.550 --> 00:22:29.910
So if you want to
learn about that stuff,

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:32.010
this is not the right
course for that.

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:34.510
If you come in and say, I
have an application domain,

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:38.450
I want to get good
results on it,

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:41.930
probably this is not the right
course, at least to start.

00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:46.070
I always recommend just prompt
a model, fine tune a model.

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:50.210
And then as a last resort,
you pre-train your own model.

00:22:50.210 --> 00:22:53.930
Because it is a pain
and it's also expensive,

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:56.370
but it's a lot of fun.

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:56.870
OK.

00:22:56.870 --> 00:23:00.850
So if you are not
taking the class,

00:23:00.850 --> 00:23:03.270
you can self-follow
along at home.

00:23:03.270 --> 00:23:06.470
So all the lecture materials
will be posted on the website.

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:06.970
Oops!

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:10.750
And they are also
recorded through CGOE.

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:12.950
So thank you, CGOE,
for doing that.

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:18.380
And later, they will be
published to YouTube.

00:23:18.380 --> 00:23:21.860
Now, of course, following at
home and watching the lectures

00:23:21.860 --> 00:23:24.320
is great, but you learn, really,
by doing the assignments.

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:29.620
So you'll have to figure out how
to motivate yourself to do that.

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:33.220
OK, so speaking of assignments,
we have five assignments.

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:38.660
The philosophy assignment is,
how do we do from scratch,

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:41.600
but not just, say,
build a language model,

00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:42.940
and that's the assignment?

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:45.760
So we don't provide
scaffolding code,

00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:47.700
but we do provide a
bunch of unit tests

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:50.300
to make sure that
whatever you're building

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:52.660
is actually correct,
so that you don't

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:56.060
get this sparse reward setting
where you submit a homework

00:23:56.060 --> 00:24:00.020
and it's either correct or not.

00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:04.020
What I recommend
is that you can--

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:07.180
the assignments are structured
so that much of the assignment

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:09.280
can actually be done
locally on your laptop.

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:11.860
You can implement it and
check for correctness.

00:24:11.860 --> 00:24:14.300
And then we are
providing a cluster

00:24:14.300 --> 00:24:17.140
so that you can do an
actual training run

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:19.940
to see what the accuracy
is, or get a bunch of GPUs

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:25.540
to actually benchmark the
performance of some kernel.

00:24:25.540 --> 00:24:29.820
And then for fun,
we will have, some

00:24:29.820 --> 00:24:34.620
leaderboards for most of
the assignments, at least.

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:37.220
And they will look
something like, well, now

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:38.980
that you've learned
about this topic,

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:40.980
I'll try to minimize
the perplexities given

00:24:40.980 --> 00:24:42.380
some of budget.

00:24:42.380 --> 00:24:47.700
And both Marcel and Herman
were masters of leaderboards

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:51.660
back in the day, which was
last year, or the year before.

00:24:51.660 --> 00:24:54.938
So if you want tips on that,
I'm sure they would be happy--

00:24:54.938 --> 00:24:57.480
actually, I don't know, if they
would tell you their secrets,

00:24:57.480 --> 00:25:00.860
but you can try.

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:05.900
OK, so last year, we were
thinking about, well,

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:07.760
what can AI do?

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:09.780
It was like, OK, well,
I mean, yes, everyone

00:25:09.780 --> 00:25:16.000
can use AI, but just
try to do your best.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:18.020
I think now, I
think, coding agents

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:20.275
have gotten so good that
they can just all solve

00:25:20.275 --> 00:25:21.400
all the assignments, right?

00:25:21.400 --> 00:25:23.980
But to state the obvious,
obviously, you're

00:25:23.980 --> 00:25:26.940
not going to learn anything
if you just feed in the PDF

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:31.540
Assignment 1 into a Cloud Code.

00:25:31.540 --> 00:25:34.500
At the same time, AI can be very
useful for answering questions

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:35.160
and tutoring.

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:41.420
So we have to find a
way to leverage AI.

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:46.960
So what we've decided to do is,
we provide you a AGENTS.md file,

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:52.140
or equivalently, a prompt which
asks the AI to be pedagogically

00:25:52.140 --> 00:25:53.260
minded.

00:25:53.260 --> 00:25:56.780
You can read more about
it in our AI policy guide.

00:25:56.780 --> 00:26:00.340
And the requirement is that
if you're going to use AI,

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:02.660
use it with this prompt.

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:05.440
And so it will answer
questions about code,

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:07.160
it will clarify
any understanding,

00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:10.400
but it won't accidentally
generate the transformer for you

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:14.020
when the homework is to
implement the transformer.

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:17.000
And this is the first year
we're trying to do this,

00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:19.340
so please try it out
and give us feedback

00:26:19.340 --> 00:26:23.140
if it's working or not working.

00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:25.380
So compute.

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:29.140
So this year we have--
thanks to Modal,

00:26:29.140 --> 00:26:31.660
they have provided us
with compute credits

00:26:31.660 --> 00:26:33.860
on their platform.

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:35.360
It's actually quite nice.

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:37.940
You can get a number of--

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:40.147
unlike last year--
well, I guess, you guys

00:26:40.147 --> 00:26:41.480
didn't take the class last year.

00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:43.940
Last year, we had a
cluster you SSH into.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:47.180
This is using more
of an API to--

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:49.860
which I was initially skeptical
of, but looking at it,

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:52.440
actually, is pretty
pleasant to use.

00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:58.220
So again, try it out and give
us feedback on how it is.

00:26:58.220 --> 00:27:04.200
We've written a guide on how
to access and use the compute.

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:07.960
OK, any questions
about course logistics?

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:17.640
OK.

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:19.420
All right, let's
talk about what we're

00:27:19.420 --> 00:27:22.460
going to cover in this class.

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:28.380
So there's, basically,
five parts mirroring

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:30.540
the five assignments
that you'll have.

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:33.220
Basic system, scaling,
laws, data, and alignment.

00:27:33.220 --> 00:27:38.020
So I'm going to now
go through each part

00:27:38.020 --> 00:27:42.100
and just give you a taste
of what you will learn.

00:27:42.100 --> 00:27:48.660
So in the basics, this is
basically the first two weeks.

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:50.940
The goal is to just be able
to train a language model

00:27:50.940 --> 00:27:53.100
and build it from scratch.

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:54.860
So the components
here are, we're

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:56.580
going to tokenize
the data, we're

00:27:56.580 --> 00:27:58.260
going to define
architecture, and then

00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:00.500
we're going to implement
optimizer and train it.

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:04.450
So then you wonder, what are
the rest of the classes for?

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:06.390
Well, we'll get to there.

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:08.770
So let's start
with tokenization.

00:28:08.770 --> 00:28:10.710
The tokenization
is really about,

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:14.830
what are the atoms that
the model operates on?

00:28:14.830 --> 00:28:17.750
And formally, a
tokenizer converts

00:28:17.750 --> 00:28:21.190
between raw inputs,
which are just bytes,

00:28:21.190 --> 00:28:26.710
and a sequence of integers,
which represent the tokens.

00:28:26.710 --> 00:28:30.870
Conceptually, it's a
segmentation of the text.

00:28:30.870 --> 00:28:33.270
We're going to talk about
the Byte-Pair Encoding,

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:36.590
BPE tokenizer,
which intuitively,

00:28:36.590 --> 00:28:41.670
breaks the input into
frequently occurring chunks.

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:45.310
And then remember, this class
is about maximizing efficiency,

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:48.350
so through an efficiency
lens, tokenization is good

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:49.730
because it takes
a long sequence,

00:28:49.730 --> 00:28:51.563
if you just think about
the raw byte stream,

00:28:51.563 --> 00:28:55.310
and reduces it into a
smaller number of tokens.

00:28:55.310 --> 00:28:58.910
But more subtly, but
maybe more importantly,

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:01.730
it allows you to do
adaptive computation.

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:06.410
So maybe some places are
actually a lot of bites,

00:29:06.410 --> 00:29:09.050
but actually, it should be
compressed into a one token,

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:12.950
whereas some of the more rare
or interesting parts of input

00:29:12.950 --> 00:29:15.510
should be left as
multiple tokens.

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:17.710
OK, we'll talk more about this.

00:29:17.710 --> 00:29:20.630
I just want to mention
that every year,

00:29:20.630 --> 00:29:24.150
I'm hoping that I don't
have to teach tokenization,

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:28.390
because the dream is to really
have an end-to-end way that

00:29:28.390 --> 00:29:30.710
directly operates on bytes.

00:29:30.710 --> 00:29:34.590
And there's been a number
of work, including recently,

00:29:34.590 --> 00:29:39.810
there's been these networks
that seems promising,

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:42.410
but so far, these have not
been scaled to the frontier.

00:29:42.410 --> 00:29:45.390
And since the frontier models
are still using tokenizers,

00:29:45.390 --> 00:29:50.190
we felt like it would be still
wise to teach tokenizers.

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:54.730
OK, so now after you
tokenize your input,

00:29:54.730 --> 00:29:56.330
you have a bunch of tokens.

00:29:56.330 --> 00:29:58.270
Now you define a model on top.

00:29:58.270 --> 00:30:00.350
And everyone, I think,
has a familiarity

00:30:00.350 --> 00:30:03.750
with the transformer.

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:07.570
And if you've taken
224n, the MLP class,

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:10.630
then you've seen transformers.

00:30:10.630 --> 00:30:14.310
Since then, I think, there
have been a lot of improvements

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:18.323
or refinements to transformers,
which I think are important.

00:30:18.323 --> 00:30:20.490
And Tatsu is going to talk
more about this in a bit.

00:30:20.490 --> 00:30:24.270
But just to run through
a set of types of things

00:30:24.270 --> 00:30:26.830
that one might have
to think about,

00:30:26.830 --> 00:30:31.750
so the activation
functions have evolved.

00:30:31.750 --> 00:30:35.070
How do you do positional
encodings has evolved.

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:40.990
How you normalize has
the different layers.

00:30:40.990 --> 00:30:43.910
Blow up has evolved.

00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:45.910
Instead of doing full
attention, there's

00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:49.470
many ways to basically reduce
the attention computation,

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:52.390
because attention is
n squared and where

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:56.230
n is a sequence length, and
that gets really expensive.

00:30:56.230 --> 00:30:58.950
So there's a bunch
of ideas around that.

00:30:58.950 --> 00:31:01.030
If you're more
ambitious, you can

00:31:01.030 --> 00:31:06.990
look at these state-space models
or equivalently linear attention

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:08.570
like Mamba and Gated DeltaNet.

00:31:08.570 --> 00:31:12.410
These have become more
popular in the last few years.

00:31:12.410 --> 00:31:15.190
And usually, some hybrid between
these models and attention

00:31:15.190 --> 00:31:17.370
seems to work quite well.

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:20.430
So, we'll be exploring
some of that.

00:31:20.430 --> 00:31:24.310
And then within the MLP
layers of the transformer,

00:31:24.310 --> 00:31:27.110
the original transformer
was just a dense MLP,

00:31:27.110 --> 00:31:31.630
and now a mixture of experts
has become the dominant paradigm

00:31:31.630 --> 00:31:34.970
for building compute
efficient transformers.

00:31:34.970 --> 00:31:37.130
So we're going to
talk about that.

00:31:37.130 --> 00:31:41.150
And of course with a
mixture of experts,

00:31:41.150 --> 00:31:42.650
it's not just
defining architecture,

00:31:42.650 --> 00:31:45.630
but we'll see that we'll also
need different techniques

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:48.310
for training the model.

00:31:48.310 --> 00:31:51.290
And then finally, perhaps
somewhat boringly,

00:31:51.290 --> 00:31:53.910
but an important
question is, what is

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:55.330
the shape of your transformer?

00:31:55.330 --> 00:31:56.480
How many layers?

00:31:56.480 --> 00:31:58.200
How many heads?

00:31:58.200 --> 00:31:59.260
What is hidden dimension?

00:31:59.260 --> 00:32:00.760
Number of experts?

00:32:00.760 --> 00:32:03.300
This might come in more as
we talk about scaling laws,

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:09.037
but setting these is actually--
seems kind of almost trivial.

00:32:09.037 --> 00:32:11.120
It's a hyperparameter, but
actually in the context

00:32:11.120 --> 00:32:17.080
of scaling and language models,
it has a huge, huge implication.

00:32:17.080 --> 00:32:20.440
So once you define your
model architecture,

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:23.240
how do you train the model?

00:32:23.240 --> 00:32:26.800
And here there's a bunch of
design decisions around the loss

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:27.920
function.

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:32.280
There's next token prediction,
which is the default.

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:37.320
But people have found that
predicting more than one token

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:41.720
seems to be helpful for
improving the model.

00:32:41.720 --> 00:32:44.360
And there's optimizers.

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:47.960
People used to use AdamW,
but increasingly, Muon

00:32:47.960 --> 00:32:52.540
has been used, especially with
some of the latest open models,

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:57.920
such as the Kimi K2 models.

00:32:57.920 --> 00:33:02.700
Initialization, which,
again, sounds kind of boring,

00:33:02.700 --> 00:33:04.440
but turns out to
have a huge impact

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:07.120
on how your ability
in the training

00:33:07.120 --> 00:33:08.740
stability of larger models.

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:10.640
Learning rate schedule.

00:33:10.640 --> 00:33:11.880
Regularization.

00:33:11.880 --> 00:33:15.480
Batch size.

00:33:15.480 --> 00:33:17.800
And then MoE specific things.

00:33:17.800 --> 00:33:20.648
So you look at this list
and you might think, well,

00:33:20.648 --> 00:33:21.940
these are just hyperparameters.

00:33:21.940 --> 00:33:24.940
I'm going to try a bunch
of different options out.

00:33:24.940 --> 00:33:30.320
But it turns out that really
being very careful about setting

00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:32.960
these hyperparameters
in a principled way

00:33:32.960 --> 00:33:36.640
will make the difference between
a run that just blows up and is

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:42.040
useless between a run that is
achieving state of the art.

00:33:42.040 --> 00:33:45.760
OK, I'll come back to that point
when we talk about scaling loss.

00:33:45.760 --> 00:33:48.760
So then in Assignment 1,
what you're going to do

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:51.520
is you're going to
implement the BPE tokenizer,

00:33:51.520 --> 00:33:54.340
implement the transformer, the
loss function, the optimizer,

00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:55.785
the whole training step.

00:33:55.785 --> 00:33:58.160
We're going to make you do a
bunch of resource accounting

00:33:58.160 --> 00:34:01.305
so you understand where
your flops are going.

00:34:01.305 --> 00:34:02.680
You're going to
train some models

00:34:02.680 --> 00:34:06.280
on these data sets like
TinyStories and OpenWebText.

00:34:06.280 --> 00:34:10.600
And then there's going
to be a leaderboard where

00:34:10.600 --> 00:34:13.280
you're going to try to
drive down perplexity

00:34:13.280 --> 00:34:15.940
as fast as you can.

00:34:15.940 --> 00:34:19.699
So for those of you familiar
with NanoGPT, speedruns,

00:34:19.699 --> 00:34:22.300
it's kind of similar to that.

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:27.233
OK, so by the end
of Assignment 1,

00:34:27.233 --> 00:34:29.400
you should be able to walk
away and build a language

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:32.360
model from scratch.

00:34:32.360 --> 00:34:36.189
So that's very exciting.

00:34:36.189 --> 00:34:39.520
If I have a high
level takeaway here,

00:34:39.520 --> 00:34:45.360
it's that while the tokenizer
and modeling and training

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:50.440
are presented as distinct
pieces, it is actually,

00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:53.480
everything is about
balancing the following.

00:34:53.480 --> 00:34:56.199
So you want expressive
models because you

00:34:56.199 --> 00:34:58.880
want to represent the
complexities of the data,

00:34:58.880 --> 00:35:04.560
but at the same time, you want
your training to be stable.

00:35:04.560 --> 00:35:08.200
And we're going to talk
a lot about how do you

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:12.000
keep the parameter and gradient
norms in this goldilocks zone

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:13.820
so they don't blow
up and don't vanish.

00:35:13.820 --> 00:35:16.040
Turns out, a lot of
training language models

00:35:16.040 --> 00:35:20.120
is about just stability.

00:35:20.120 --> 00:35:24.320
And then finally, efficiency,
which is somewhat more

00:35:24.320 --> 00:35:25.400
straightforward.

00:35:25.400 --> 00:35:27.800
You just make it run
fast on hardware.

00:35:27.800 --> 00:35:30.320
But you're going to see
interesting things like,

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:33.600
if we change the architecture,
a lot of the architecture

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:37.300
decisions are, well, we can
make it faster by, let's say,

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:41.860
reducing the projecting of
a low dimensional space.

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:44.120
But then the question
is, does it work as well?

00:35:44.120 --> 00:35:48.490
And so making those trade
offs is something that--

00:35:48.490 --> 00:35:53.170
is the name of the game here.

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:58.930
So in Assignment 2, we're
going to dive more deeply

00:35:58.930 --> 00:36:03.090
into systems.

00:36:03.090 --> 00:36:05.828
And the goal here is just to
get most out of your hardware.

00:36:05.828 --> 00:36:07.370
So we're going to
talk about kernels,

00:36:07.370 --> 00:36:10.370
how you parallelize
across multiple GPUs,

00:36:10.370 --> 00:36:12.410
and how you do inference.

00:36:12.410 --> 00:36:15.530
So the basics,
which we're actually

00:36:15.530 --> 00:36:20.570
going to start on next lecture,
I mentioned resource accounting,

00:36:20.570 --> 00:36:26.810
and I think you've all
probably built models before.

00:36:26.810 --> 00:36:33.730
But this is really about keeping
track of where all the flops go

00:36:33.730 --> 00:36:35.415
and where all the
memory is being spent.

00:36:35.415 --> 00:36:37.290
So we're going to spend
some time, basically,

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:39.410
doing the resource accounting.

00:36:39.410 --> 00:36:43.830
We're going to see this
formula that comes up,

00:36:43.830 --> 00:36:46.970
how many flops does
training SMDB on model

00:36:46.970 --> 00:36:48.230
on 1 trillion tokens?

00:36:48.230 --> 00:36:51.410
Well, it's six times
n times D roughly.

00:36:51.410 --> 00:36:53.850
And where does that come from?

00:36:53.850 --> 00:36:58.130
And then we're going to
look at the hardware.

00:36:58.130 --> 00:37:02.250
And here's a cartoon
I picture of what

00:37:02.250 --> 00:37:05.530
to remark about hardware,
is that, your memory is not

00:37:05.530 --> 00:37:07.490
where your compute
is, and you have

00:37:07.490 --> 00:37:12.690
to move either your parameters
or activations from the memory

00:37:12.690 --> 00:37:15.490
to compute, do the
compute, and move it back.

00:37:15.490 --> 00:37:20.250
And that often is
the bottleneck.

00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:23.810
So for example, B200, which
we'll have the opportunity

00:37:23.810 --> 00:37:30.370
to play with, it has 2.25
petaFLOPS per second if bf16.

00:37:30.370 --> 00:37:33.990
And has 8 terabytes
second of memory.

00:37:33.990 --> 00:37:35.410
So what does that mean?

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:36.530
I think when we--

00:37:36.530 --> 00:37:39.190
I think I'll do
this next lecture.

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:42.650
We're going to break this
down and use this information

00:37:42.650 --> 00:37:46.050
to do some calculations
and see how

00:37:46.050 --> 00:37:49.207
long different types of
algorithms will need to take.

00:37:49.207 --> 00:37:51.290
We're going to talk about
roofline analysis, which

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:55.170
allows us to understand whether
a computation is bottlenecked

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:57.290
by either a compute or memory.

00:37:57.290 --> 00:38:02.410
In general, it is memory.

00:38:02.410 --> 00:38:05.430
And then talk a little bit about
benchmarking and profiling.

00:38:08.896 --> 00:38:13.390
So here's what a
DGX B200 looks like.

00:38:13.390 --> 00:38:15.330
You have 8 GPUs.

00:38:15.330 --> 00:38:19.570
They're connected via MV link.

00:38:19.570 --> 00:38:23.248
And then if you have many--
if you have a thousand GPUs,

00:38:23.248 --> 00:38:24.790
then you would have
multiple of these

00:38:24.790 --> 00:38:28.330
and they're connected either
InfiniBand or ethernet.

00:38:28.330 --> 00:38:32.750
So the next two parts when we
talk about system is kernel.

00:38:32.750 --> 00:38:36.010
So a kernel is basically a
function that runs on the GPU.

00:38:36.010 --> 00:38:38.210
And when you're just
using plain PyTorch,

00:38:38.210 --> 00:38:42.530
all the PyTorch
primitives actually

00:38:42.530 --> 00:38:45.490
correspond to launching
particular kernels, which

00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:47.650
are built in.

00:38:47.650 --> 00:38:54.170
So you're already using
kernels, whether you it or not.

00:38:54.170 --> 00:38:59.670
But the point is that, for
certain types of computation,

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:02.570
if you look at it, you can
actually write custom kernels

00:39:02.570 --> 00:39:05.810
to make the GPUs go faster.

00:39:05.810 --> 00:39:10.170
And the main principle here
is organizing the compute

00:39:10.170 --> 00:39:11.670
to minimize data movement.

00:39:11.670 --> 00:39:19.550
So remember, this picture moving
data from memory is expensive.

00:39:19.550 --> 00:39:21.970
So you want to try
to minimize that.

00:39:21.970 --> 00:39:24.810
So just as a simple
example, suppose

00:39:24.810 --> 00:39:27.210
you wanted to compute
A and B. So often,

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:31.810
you would have to read from
your high bandwidth memory, HBM,

00:39:31.810 --> 00:39:34.350
compute it, write it
back, and then read again,

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:36.410
compute it, write it back.

00:39:36.410 --> 00:39:39.850
So then, you're basically
sending the data back and forth

00:39:39.850 --> 00:39:40.740
twice.

00:39:40.740 --> 00:39:44.840
And there's this idea called
fusion, where you read it once,

00:39:44.840 --> 00:39:47.340
do both of the computations,
and you write it back.

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:52.060
And that will save
you a lot of time.

00:39:52.060 --> 00:39:53.740
So that's operator fusion.

00:39:53.740 --> 00:39:59.180
Tiling is a more sophisticated
variant around the same idea.

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:03.940
There's also-- GPUs have also
gotten a lot more complicated.

00:40:03.940 --> 00:40:05.740
And I'm not sure how
many of these details

00:40:05.740 --> 00:40:07.573
we'll have time to get
into, but at least we

00:40:07.573 --> 00:40:12.040
want to expose you to some of
the peculiarities, I would say,

00:40:12.040 --> 00:40:15.580
of GPUs and give you an
appreciation for the types

00:40:15.580 --> 00:40:17.860
of things that one has
to consider in order

00:40:17.860 --> 00:40:20.740
to squeeze as much juice
out of them as possible.

00:40:20.740 --> 00:40:25.060
And we'll write some
kernels in and Triton.

00:40:25.060 --> 00:40:28.760
So what happens if you
have thousands of GPUs?

00:40:31.820 --> 00:40:36.500
So the principle of minimized
data movement is still the same.

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:41.620
The only thing is that moving
data between different GPUs

00:40:41.620 --> 00:40:44.420
is even more expensive.

00:40:44.420 --> 00:40:47.820
We're going to talk about how
these very classic collective

00:40:47.820 --> 00:40:51.020
operations, gather and
reduce and all-reduce

00:40:51.020 --> 00:40:57.780
are the way to think about,
basically, distributed training.

00:40:57.780 --> 00:41:01.440
The general game is that we
have these model parameters,

00:41:01.440 --> 00:41:04.720
We have activations, gradients
and optimizer states,

00:41:04.720 --> 00:41:08.580
and they need to be sharded
or split across multiple GPUs.

00:41:08.580 --> 00:41:10.580
And of course, you need
to bring the right data

00:41:10.580 --> 00:41:13.472
to the right nodes to make
the compute and write it back.

00:41:13.472 --> 00:41:15.180
So there's a whole
kind of orchestration,

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:18.020
and how to do that
efficiently is going

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:23.540
to be the topic of this unit.

00:41:23.540 --> 00:41:26.120
And there's multiple
ways to shard.

00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:28.640
You can shard by
splitting up your data,

00:41:28.640 --> 00:41:30.980
splitting up your model,
splitting up different layers

00:41:30.980 --> 00:41:33.200
in the model, splitting
up the sequences,

00:41:33.200 --> 00:41:34.762
splitting up between experts.

00:41:34.762 --> 00:41:36.220
And we'll talk
about the trade offs

00:41:36.220 --> 00:41:38.560
that come with each of these.

00:41:38.560 --> 00:41:39.060
OK.

00:41:39.060 --> 00:41:43.460
And then finally, we're going
to talk about inference.

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:46.780
Which, as I mentioned,
is growing in importance.

00:41:46.780 --> 00:41:49.640
So the goal of inference is
to actually use the model.

00:41:49.640 --> 00:41:52.700
So minor detail here.

00:41:52.700 --> 00:41:55.743
So inference is, of course,
you need to use inference

00:41:55.743 --> 00:41:57.160
when you're chatting
with a model,

00:41:57.160 --> 00:42:01.700
but it also is useful for
reinforcement learning.

00:42:01.700 --> 00:42:05.120
It's useful for
doing the rollouts.

00:42:05.120 --> 00:42:07.880
Test-time compute, generating
synthetic data, evaluation.

00:42:07.880 --> 00:42:11.300
So inference is a
very critical part

00:42:11.300 --> 00:42:16.120
of what it means to do
language modeling work.

00:42:16.120 --> 00:42:19.660
So we're probably not
going to spend as much time

00:42:19.660 --> 00:42:23.060
as I like on this because the
course is already kind of filled

00:42:23.060 --> 00:42:26.180
up, but we'll see
what we can do.

00:42:26.180 --> 00:42:28.860
There was some discussion
around whether we

00:42:28.860 --> 00:42:32.280
should make you write inference
from scratch, but we'll see.

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:37.580
So the way to think
about inference

00:42:37.580 --> 00:42:40.140
is that there's two phases,
a prefill and a decode.

00:42:40.140 --> 00:42:41.660
In the prefill,
You take the prompt

00:42:41.660 --> 00:42:44.020
and then you feed all
the tokens forward

00:42:44.020 --> 00:42:46.280
and build key value pairs.

00:42:46.280 --> 00:42:49.900
This is very much like
what happens in training.

00:42:49.900 --> 00:42:53.500
And then in the
decoding part, tokens

00:42:53.500 --> 00:42:55.380
are generated one at a time.

00:42:55.380 --> 00:43:00.800
And this is the part that
becomes quickly memory bound,

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:03.960
and this is why
inference is hard.

00:43:03.960 --> 00:43:08.700
And so there's many things you
can do to speed up inference.

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:12.520
You can try to use a cheaper
model by pruning a larger model

00:43:12.520 --> 00:43:14.548
or you can quantize,
you can distill.

00:43:14.548 --> 00:43:16.840
You can use this technique
called speculative decoding,

00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:20.340
where you use a cheaper
model to run ahead and guess

00:43:20.340 --> 00:43:21.680
a bunch of tokens.

00:43:21.680 --> 00:43:23.460
And now, you use the
full model, which

00:43:23.460 --> 00:43:28.380
can operate on those tokens in
parallel to see if it's good.

00:43:28.380 --> 00:43:31.140
And if you got lucky, then you
can accept all those tokens

00:43:31.140 --> 00:43:33.180
and you're much
faster than if you're

00:43:33.180 --> 00:43:35.220
doing one token at a time.

00:43:35.220 --> 00:43:38.020
And of course, you can
do systems optimizations.

00:43:38.020 --> 00:43:40.700
There's a bunch of kernels
that are designed specifically

00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:41.940
for inference.

00:43:41.940 --> 00:43:44.700
And then one of the interesting
things about inference

00:43:44.700 --> 00:43:47.100
is that, if you're
running a service,

00:43:47.100 --> 00:43:51.940
queries are coming at you at
potentially different times,

00:43:51.940 --> 00:43:54.780
and then you have to figure
out how to batch them up.

00:43:54.780 --> 00:43:57.540
Whereas in training,
you basically

00:43:57.540 --> 00:43:59.700
already define your
batches and everything

00:43:59.700 --> 00:44:02.180
is much more predictable.

00:44:02.180 --> 00:44:05.820
So in Assignment
2, there's going

00:44:05.820 --> 00:44:10.420
to be implementing
kernels in Triton

00:44:10.420 --> 00:44:14.460
and doing some of
parallel training.

00:44:14.460 --> 00:44:16.600
The details here
might actually change.

00:44:16.600 --> 00:44:24.020
As the CAAs have grand plans
for revamping the systems.

00:44:24.020 --> 00:44:27.020
So the assignment
might look a bit

00:44:27.020 --> 00:44:30.500
different from last
year's, but it will cover

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:33.190
roughly the same material.

00:44:33.190 --> 00:44:36.990
One thing I will mention is that
there's this wonderful book out

00:44:36.990 --> 00:44:39.990
of some Google people called
How to Scale Your Model,

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:43.070
and I think it's really
nice for providing

00:44:43.070 --> 00:44:45.870
a conceptual understanding
of roofline analysis,

00:44:45.870 --> 00:44:51.030
and transformer math, and
doing LLMs conceptually.

00:44:51.030 --> 00:44:53.990
So I highly recommend
you take a look at that.

00:44:53.990 --> 00:44:58.890
Now, the only thing is that it's
from Google, so it's about TPUs.

00:44:58.890 --> 00:45:03.050
But a lot of the high
level concepts are similar.

00:45:03.050 --> 00:45:06.330
And now, they have a new chapter
on how to think about GPUs.

00:45:09.510 --> 00:45:12.225
OK.

00:45:12.225 --> 00:45:15.850
So the third assignment
is about scaling laws.

00:45:15.850 --> 00:45:19.990
So by now, you've
trained a language model.

00:45:19.990 --> 00:45:24.790
You can make it go really
fast by optimizing kernels

00:45:24.790 --> 00:45:25.670
in parallel.

00:45:25.670 --> 00:45:28.550
Now, you want to scale up.

00:45:28.550 --> 00:45:30.710
So how do you scale up?

00:45:30.710 --> 00:45:32.630
So imagine the
following setting.

00:45:32.630 --> 00:45:36.870
If you had 1e25 FLOPs, so this
is tens of millions of dollars

00:45:36.870 --> 00:45:42.030
of compute, what
model would you train?

00:45:42.030 --> 00:45:45.670
So this is, I think,
a daunting task

00:45:45.670 --> 00:45:48.510
because if you mess up,
well, that's a lot of money

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:49.990
down the drain.

00:45:49.990 --> 00:45:54.830
And you can't do your,
probably, typical hyperparameter

00:45:54.830 --> 00:45:58.950
tuning at that scale because
you only get to train one model.

00:45:58.950 --> 00:46:01.870
And so this is the
key problem that you

00:46:01.870 --> 00:46:05.950
have to deal with in large
language model training

00:46:05.950 --> 00:46:08.750
that you don't have to really
deal with if you're just fine

00:46:08.750 --> 00:46:11.870
tuning a model or doing
small scale stuff.

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:14.310
And so the key
conceptual shift here

00:46:14.310 --> 00:46:16.830
is that we shouldn't
think about a single model

00:46:16.830 --> 00:46:20.090
that we're training, but really
think about a scaling recipe.

00:46:20.090 --> 00:46:24.270
And a scaling recipe is a
mapping from a FLOP budget,

00:46:24.270 --> 00:46:28.130
let's say 1e25 or 1e24, to
a set of hyperparameters.

00:46:28.130 --> 00:46:30.070
Basically a config file.

00:46:30.070 --> 00:46:35.230
And for a given scaling
recipe, what we will do

00:46:35.230 --> 00:46:40.390
is run a bunch of experiments
to compute the loss that you

00:46:40.390 --> 00:46:44.090
get at smaller scales, and
then you fit a scaling law,

00:46:44.090 --> 00:46:47.823
and that enables you to predict
the loss at a target scale.

00:46:47.823 --> 00:46:49.490
So maybe you run some
small experiments,

00:46:49.490 --> 00:46:54.150
you fit a scaling law,
and then you predict out

00:46:54.150 --> 00:46:56.790
what you're going to
get at a larger scale.

00:46:56.790 --> 00:46:58.730
So that's the primitive.

00:46:58.730 --> 00:47:01.830
Now using this, what
you can do is now

00:47:01.830 --> 00:47:05.170
you can optimize
the scaling recipe,

00:47:05.170 --> 00:47:08.830
targeting a larger scale using
smaller scale experiments, which

00:47:08.830 --> 00:47:09.910
is wonderful.

00:47:09.910 --> 00:47:12.230
And second of all, you
can predict the loss

00:47:12.230 --> 00:47:17.030
that you're going to, in
theory, achieve before actually

00:47:17.030 --> 00:47:19.150
running the experiment.

00:47:19.150 --> 00:47:23.210
Which allows you
to go raise money.

00:47:23.210 --> 00:47:26.530
And you say, well, look, I ran
the small scale experiments,

00:47:26.530 --> 00:47:29.930
and I think I can get really--
like, a GPD-5 level model.

00:47:29.930 --> 00:47:32.850
Please give me a lot of money
so I can train that model.

00:47:35.390 --> 00:47:40.430
But one thing I think is
maybe another misconception

00:47:40.430 --> 00:47:43.890
is that scaling laws
are not laws of nature.

00:47:43.890 --> 00:47:46.790
They don't just
happen automatically.

00:47:46.790 --> 00:47:49.470
You have to will
them into existence.

00:47:49.470 --> 00:47:52.430
And this happens by
careful construction

00:47:52.430 --> 00:47:54.510
of a scaling recipe.

00:47:54.510 --> 00:47:57.950
And the scaling recipe,
remember, it has to extrapolate.

00:47:57.950 --> 00:48:00.550
So what this typically
means is that you

00:48:00.550 --> 00:48:02.590
have a sequence of
hyperparameters which,

00:48:02.590 --> 00:48:05.270
say, as the scale increases,
maybe the learning rate

00:48:05.270 --> 00:48:08.350
is a constant, maybe it
drops, maybe the batch size

00:48:08.350 --> 00:48:09.790
increases by how much.

00:48:09.790 --> 00:48:11.910
And these are things
that a scaling

00:48:11.910 --> 00:48:15.030
recipe has to figure out.

00:48:15.030 --> 00:48:18.150
And so you parameterize.

00:48:18.150 --> 00:48:23.440
So then in order to get these
predictable scaling laws,

00:48:23.440 --> 00:48:25.480
one thing that you actually
have to think about

00:48:25.480 --> 00:48:27.880
is how do you
parameterize the model

00:48:27.880 --> 00:48:31.080
in a way to get what's called
hyperparameter transfer?

00:48:31.080 --> 00:48:34.160
Meaning that the hyperparameters
you use at small scale

00:48:34.160 --> 00:48:36.160
are either the ones that
you use at larger scale

00:48:36.160 --> 00:48:38.200
or are predictable
functions of it.

00:48:38.200 --> 00:48:42.480
Because if every scale, your
learning rate is sometimes 1e-5,

00:48:42.480 --> 00:48:45.920
and sometimes 1e-4, then
you're not going to be able

00:48:45.920 --> 00:48:48.960
to magically guess the right
learning rate when you are

00:48:48.960 --> 00:48:50.720
at larger scale.

00:48:50.720 --> 00:48:55.000
So one shift in thinking is
that predictability is actually

00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:56.935
at least as important
as optimality.

00:48:56.935 --> 00:48:58.560
So you normally think,
oh, we're trying

00:48:58.560 --> 00:49:02.120
to optimize for
efficiency here, and we

00:49:02.120 --> 00:49:05.660
want to hyperparameter tune
and make things optimal.

00:49:05.660 --> 00:49:08.440
And yes, you do want to
do that, but you also

00:49:08.440 --> 00:49:11.600
want this predictability so
that you don't get surprised

00:49:11.600 --> 00:49:13.120
at larger scale.

00:49:13.120 --> 00:49:17.320
So this is state setting.

00:49:17.320 --> 00:49:19.400
The actual scaling laws
we're going to look at

00:49:19.400 --> 00:49:22.720
are fairly classic.

00:49:22.720 --> 00:49:28.060
So these are-- some of you might
have seen this idea of, well,

00:49:28.060 --> 00:49:31.960
if I give you a FLOPs
budget, should you train--

00:49:31.960 --> 00:49:34.880
how should you balance training
a larger model versus training

00:49:34.880 --> 00:49:36.000
on more tokens?

00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:38.440
And this is where the classic
compute optimal scaling laws

00:49:38.440 --> 00:49:42.200
from Kaplan et Al and the
so-called Chinchilla scaling

00:49:42.200 --> 00:49:43.520
laws comes in.

00:49:43.520 --> 00:49:50.920
The basic idea is that
you for each FLOPs budget,

00:49:50.920 --> 00:49:56.520
so let's say 6e18
all the way to 3e21,

00:49:56.520 --> 00:50:05.280
you sweep across different model
sizes and you choose the best

00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:05.860
one.

00:50:05.860 --> 00:50:08.200
So think about
minimizing each of these.

00:50:08.200 --> 00:50:12.680
And then you fit a curve that
allows you to, basically,

00:50:12.680 --> 00:50:16.240
predict the number of
parameters given a FLOPs budget.

00:50:16.240 --> 00:50:19.985
And if you're lucky, it will
rely roughly on the line.

00:50:19.985 --> 00:50:21.360
And if you're
unlucky, it's going

00:50:21.360 --> 00:50:23.777
to be all over the place, which
means that you should have

00:50:23.777 --> 00:50:27.600
no confidence that you're going
to be able to predict reliably.

00:50:27.600 --> 00:50:35.020
And so the upshot of
this, this is quite crude,

00:50:35.020 --> 00:50:42.120
but a rule of thumb is, the 20
times the number of parameters

00:50:42.120 --> 00:50:45.700
is the number of data
points you should train on.

00:50:45.700 --> 00:50:48.840
So a 70B parameter model should
be trained on roughly 1.4

00:50:48.840 --> 00:50:50.820
trillion tokens.

00:50:50.820 --> 00:50:53.300
Of course, depending on the
data set and architecture,

00:50:53.300 --> 00:50:56.640
this number will actually vary.

00:50:56.640 --> 00:51:01.040
Also, this doesn't take you
into account the inference cost.

00:51:01.040 --> 00:51:03.220
A lot of models
these days are small,

00:51:03.220 --> 00:51:04.880
but they're trained
on way more tokens

00:51:04.880 --> 00:51:07.040
than is compute optimal
because you want a smaller

00:51:07.040 --> 00:51:09.960
model for inference reasons.

00:51:09.960 --> 00:51:14.400
So one fun thing that we've
been doing in the Marin project

00:51:14.400 --> 00:51:16.380
is pre-registering our results.

00:51:16.380 --> 00:51:20.840
So we fit a bunch
of scaling plots

00:51:20.840 --> 00:51:22.660
at different compute budgets.

00:51:22.660 --> 00:51:27.720
We fit a scaling law, and we
basically made these predictions

00:51:27.720 --> 00:51:30.520
out to 1E22 FLOPs.

00:51:30.520 --> 00:51:31.860
This one is actually training.

00:51:31.860 --> 00:51:34.520
If you go to the Marin
website, you can follow along.

00:51:34.520 --> 00:51:37.700
It should actually be done
maybe as early as tonight.

00:51:37.700 --> 00:51:41.240
So maybe on Wednesday I'll
report back on how we did

00:51:41.240 --> 00:51:43.720
and see how we measure--

00:51:43.720 --> 00:51:47.640
how we match the
pre-registered loss.

00:51:47.640 --> 00:51:50.120
So the idea here is that
we made a prediction

00:51:50.120 --> 00:51:52.600
on if we were to train this
large model, which we've never

00:51:52.600 --> 00:51:53.640
trained before.

00:51:53.640 --> 00:51:56.040
If we can predict how
well it's going to do,

00:51:56.040 --> 00:52:00.040
then that's really nice.

00:52:00.040 --> 00:52:05.120
So in Assignment 3, I
think this is either fun

00:52:05.120 --> 00:52:08.278
or actually, it's a fun--

00:52:08.278 --> 00:52:09.820
let's just say it's
a fun assignment.

00:52:09.820 --> 00:52:11.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:52:11.600 --> 00:52:16.610
So we're going to define this
training API, which basically,

00:52:16.610 --> 00:52:19.690
you give us hyperparameters, and
we're going to give you a loss

00:52:19.690 --> 00:52:20.530
back.

00:52:20.530 --> 00:52:22.090
So what we're going
to try to do is

00:52:22.090 --> 00:52:25.868
simulate what happens if you
could do a lot of training runs.

00:52:25.868 --> 00:52:28.410
And of course, we don't have
enough compute to actually allow

00:52:28.410 --> 00:52:33.410
everyone to train their
own API model or anything.

00:52:33.410 --> 00:52:36.690
So basically, we trained
a bunch of models offline,

00:52:36.690 --> 00:52:39.290
and we, basically,
provide this cache.

00:52:39.290 --> 00:52:41.810
So it looks like
you're training.

00:52:41.810 --> 00:52:45.130
And what you're going to
do is submit training jobs.

00:52:45.130 --> 00:52:48.963
You give us a config, basically,
we give you a loss back

00:52:48.963 --> 00:52:50.630
and then you could
do whatever you want.

00:52:50.630 --> 00:52:56.110
We would recommend you fit
scaling laws to these points.

00:52:56.110 --> 00:52:57.630
And then you extrapolate.

00:52:57.630 --> 00:53:01.530
And then we give you a
budget, and we basically

00:53:01.530 --> 00:53:04.610
evaluate how well
your model landed.

00:53:04.610 --> 00:53:06.170
So it is meant to--

00:53:06.170 --> 00:53:09.450
I was going to say, it's meant
to replicate the high stress

00:53:09.450 --> 00:53:14.830
scenarios if you actually had
a budget like $100 million

00:53:14.830 --> 00:53:16.730
that you need to spend.

00:53:16.730 --> 00:53:21.010
You have to be very careful
about how you spend this.

00:53:21.010 --> 00:53:23.310
Of course, this is low stakes.

00:53:28.825 --> 00:53:33.510
So at this point, you will
have, you train a model,

00:53:33.510 --> 00:53:38.050
how to make it fast,
you know to scale up.

00:53:38.050 --> 00:53:39.410
Now, what's missing?

00:53:39.410 --> 00:53:42.410
What do you train the model on?

00:53:42.410 --> 00:53:44.850
And that's going to be
the subject of the data

00:53:44.850 --> 00:53:50.930
section, which is arguably one
of the most important things

00:53:50.930 --> 00:53:55.850
because data quality basically
specifies how good your model is

00:53:55.850 --> 00:53:56.570
going to be.

00:53:56.570 --> 00:54:01.130
One way to also frame
it is, what do you

00:54:01.130 --> 00:54:02.770
want your model to do?

00:54:02.770 --> 00:54:07.990
Data, basically, reflects
what your model wants.

00:54:07.990 --> 00:54:10.850
So do you want to speak
multiple languages?

00:54:10.850 --> 00:54:13.590
Be good at having
a conversation?

00:54:13.590 --> 00:54:17.890
Do you want to run long
agentic coding tasks?

00:54:17.890 --> 00:54:21.410
And so part of
that is also going

00:54:21.410 --> 00:54:25.490
to-- so we're going to start by
talking about evaluation, which,

00:54:25.490 --> 00:54:28.130
basically, defines the
capabilities that you'd

00:54:28.130 --> 00:54:30.970
like your model to have.

00:54:30.970 --> 00:54:34.850
One thing we'll talk
about is, evaluation

00:54:34.850 --> 00:54:38.010
is a fairly deep topic.

00:54:38.010 --> 00:54:42.370
It's not just about
running on some benchmarks.

00:54:42.370 --> 00:54:46.550
There are internal evaluation
metrics for model development.

00:54:46.550 --> 00:54:52.130
And what matters here is that
smoothness across scales so

00:54:52.130 --> 00:54:55.530
that there-- remember, we
want things to be predictable.

00:54:55.530 --> 00:54:57.030
Relative performance matters.

00:54:57.030 --> 00:54:59.010
You don't care,
necessarily, how well

00:54:59.010 --> 00:55:04.107
this does in absolute
terms, because, let's say,

00:55:04.107 --> 00:55:05.690
a perplexity number
is just like, what

00:55:05.690 --> 00:55:12.290
is a perplexity of 1.2 on some
held out data really mean?

00:55:12.290 --> 00:55:14.470
And then there's
external metrics.

00:55:14.470 --> 00:55:19.490
These are things that you
report to your customers

00:55:19.490 --> 00:55:23.290
or your reviewers or whoever
you're presenting your thing to.

00:55:23.290 --> 00:55:26.170
And here, ecological
validity really matters.

00:55:26.170 --> 00:55:28.830
And I think sometimes these
two things get conflated,

00:55:28.830 --> 00:55:31.970
but I think they really
serve two distinct purposes.

00:55:31.970 --> 00:55:33.890
And so you can think
about perplexity

00:55:33.890 --> 00:55:37.090
as something that
is really helpful

00:55:37.090 --> 00:55:38.810
for internal development.

00:55:38.810 --> 00:55:42.290
And still to this
day, perplexity

00:55:42.290 --> 00:55:47.890
is a very good way of capturing
the intrinsic quality of a model

00:55:47.890 --> 00:55:49.410
without getting--

00:55:49.410 --> 00:55:51.330
worrying about benchmarking.

00:55:51.330 --> 00:55:57.330
Now, there's a separate question
of what you run your evals on.

00:55:57.330 --> 00:55:59.870
And the recommended
thing is, well,

00:55:59.870 --> 00:56:02.170
if you have some data that's
not in the internet, that

00:56:02.170 --> 00:56:05.670
would be good because you
can avoid contamination.

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:06.170
OK.

00:56:06.170 --> 00:56:10.690
So and then there's
advanced use cases

00:56:10.690 --> 00:56:17.530
which are more representative
of external facing use cases.

00:56:17.530 --> 00:56:21.290
So one thing to also note
is that language models

00:56:21.290 --> 00:56:23.890
are purportedly very
general purpose.

00:56:23.890 --> 00:56:27.090
So it's only fitting
that we actually

00:56:27.090 --> 00:56:30.290
need a very diverse
set of evaluations.

00:56:30.290 --> 00:56:33.770
And I would always recommend
having many evaluations

00:56:33.770 --> 00:56:36.230
that look at-- you can average
them into a single number,

00:56:36.230 --> 00:56:40.530
but often that average conflates
a lot of different things.

00:56:40.530 --> 00:56:42.710
So now after we
set up the evals,

00:56:42.710 --> 00:56:45.810
we know what we're building.

00:56:45.810 --> 00:56:48.370
How do you get the data?

00:56:48.370 --> 00:56:53.690
Well, first thing is that data
does not just fall from the sky.

00:56:53.690 --> 00:56:57.050
It has to be actively curated.

00:56:57.050 --> 00:57:00.947
Often, I think,
especially in classes

00:57:00.947 --> 00:57:02.530
and also in research,
sometimes you're

00:57:02.530 --> 00:57:04.590
just given a data set,
and then it's like,

00:57:04.590 --> 00:57:07.160
OK, well, now, I do
stuff on the data set.

00:57:07.160 --> 00:57:09.460
But a lot of language
models, especially

00:57:09.460 --> 00:57:11.400
if you want to collect
these large data sets,

00:57:11.400 --> 00:57:14.300
you have to go and
actively look at it.

00:57:14.300 --> 00:57:17.020
So web pages are crawled
from the internet.

00:57:17.020 --> 00:57:20.360
There's books, I guess, which
is controversial at this point,

00:57:20.360 --> 00:57:23.800
but arXiv papers,
GitHub code, and so on.

00:57:23.800 --> 00:57:27.280
This is an old figure
from the pile from 2021.

00:57:27.280 --> 00:57:30.100
And you can see, language
modeling data sets

00:57:30.100 --> 00:57:33.060
are fairly diverse.

00:57:33.060 --> 00:57:34.620
There's, especially
these days, I

00:57:34.620 --> 00:57:38.900
think, a lot of contention
around, is it fair use

00:57:38.900 --> 00:57:41.500
to train on copyrighted data?

00:57:41.500 --> 00:57:44.920
Maybe sometimes you have
to license data and so on.

00:57:44.920 --> 00:57:47.820
So there's legal issues
around data which, I think,

00:57:47.820 --> 00:57:50.180
are quite important.

00:57:50.180 --> 00:57:53.900
For example, a lot of GitHub
code doesn't have a license.

00:57:53.900 --> 00:57:55.600
So how do you interpret that?

00:57:55.600 --> 00:57:58.460
Do you assume it's permissive,
or do you be conservative

00:57:58.460 --> 00:58:01.780
and assume it's not permissive?

00:58:01.780 --> 00:58:09.100
So there's also-- the fact is
that data is not even text.

00:58:09.100 --> 00:58:12.660
It's either HTML or PDFs,
or a code is directories.

00:58:12.660 --> 00:58:16.420
And this requires processing
to turn it into actual text

00:58:16.420 --> 00:58:18.260
to be usable for training.

00:58:18.260 --> 00:58:21.580
So that's the topic
of data processing.

00:58:21.580 --> 00:58:24.460
There's a few steps
that has to happen here.

00:58:24.460 --> 00:58:30.540
Transformation, converting
some nontexting in a text.

00:58:30.540 --> 00:58:34.700
Filtering, keeping
only the good stuff.

00:58:34.700 --> 00:58:39.060
If the random internet document
in Common Crawl is extremely bad

00:58:39.060 --> 00:58:44.180
and you don't want to
train on it, most likely.

00:58:44.180 --> 00:58:46.980
You want to deduplicate.

00:58:46.980 --> 00:58:49.380
There's multiple
sources, or how do you

00:58:49.380 --> 00:58:51.900
combine the different sources?

00:58:51.900 --> 00:58:56.140
And then finally, more recently,
there's been a lot of work

00:58:56.140 --> 00:58:58.580
on generating
synthetic data, which

00:58:58.580 --> 00:59:02.260
could mean taking the real
data and just rewriting it

00:59:02.260 --> 00:59:05.980
into things that are more the
downstream task or just more

00:59:05.980 --> 00:59:09.580
Wikipedia like, or
whatever you want.

00:59:09.580 --> 00:59:12.940
So this is an active
area of research.

00:59:12.940 --> 00:59:16.960
Also, data can be used
both for pretraining.

00:59:16.960 --> 00:59:18.460
Something called
mid training, which

00:59:18.460 --> 00:59:20.780
is usually the high
quality data that you

00:59:20.780 --> 00:59:25.220
put at the end of
the pretraining step.

00:59:25.220 --> 00:59:27.700
And this includes
long context data

00:59:27.700 --> 00:59:31.500
such as maybe BigCode
repositories or books.

00:59:31.500 --> 00:59:34.180
And then finally, there's
post-training data,

00:59:34.180 --> 00:59:39.540
which are maybe conversations
or agentic traces with tool

00:59:39.540 --> 00:59:40.820
calling.

00:59:40.820 --> 00:59:45.140
So in Assignment 4,
we're going to make

00:59:45.140 --> 00:59:51.780
you start with a very raw
corpus, like a rock web crawl,

00:59:51.780 --> 00:59:57.900
and do all the work to
filter and to dedupe,

00:59:57.900 --> 01:00:00.860
and make the data clean.

01:00:00.860 --> 01:00:04.470
So this is, I would say--

01:00:04.470 --> 01:00:08.620
I don't know if I would call it
not fun, but it is certainly,

01:00:08.620 --> 01:00:13.040
I think, a lot of what
people would call dirty work.

01:00:13.040 --> 01:00:15.900
But that is, I think,
an important part

01:00:15.900 --> 01:00:18.380
of building a language model
from scratch so you have

01:00:18.380 --> 01:00:20.860
to get the full experience.

01:00:20.860 --> 01:00:25.940
So finally, alignment.

01:00:25.940 --> 01:00:30.000
So far we've, basically, trained
a model using full supervision.

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:33.260
Predict the next token
or the next few tokens.

01:00:33.260 --> 01:00:38.060
Now at this point, the model
should already be reasonable,

01:00:38.060 --> 01:00:43.260
but we can improve it further
by using weak supervision.

01:00:43.260 --> 01:00:44.520
And why weak supervision?

01:00:44.520 --> 01:00:48.500
It's because sometimes
it's easier to critique

01:00:48.500 --> 01:00:49.920
than it is to generate.

01:00:49.920 --> 01:00:52.740
So you can't always
have data that says,

01:00:52.740 --> 01:00:54.600
this is the right
response to this prompt,

01:00:54.600 --> 01:00:58.190
but maybe you can have a way of
specifying what good looks like.

01:00:58.190 --> 01:01:01.670
So then, the basic template
is that you generate responses

01:01:01.670 --> 01:01:02.410
from the model.

01:01:02.410 --> 01:01:06.650
You score them either with a
human or verify or a LM judge,

01:01:06.650 --> 01:01:09.950
and then you update the model
to prefer better responses.

01:01:09.950 --> 01:01:13.790
This can be instantiated either
through various RL algorithms

01:01:13.790 --> 01:01:19.270
such as PPO or GRPO, or in a
simpler, for preference data,

01:01:19.270 --> 01:01:22.150
DPO.

01:01:22.150 --> 01:01:29.030
So the challenges around
RL are that RL algorithms

01:01:29.030 --> 01:01:32.510
are unstable and hard to tune.

01:01:32.510 --> 01:01:36.190
Some of you probably know this
from firsthand experience.

01:01:36.190 --> 01:01:39.930
Personally, I prefer to keep
things as much in the fold

01:01:39.930 --> 01:01:41.890
so we supervise the case
as long as possible.

01:01:41.890 --> 01:01:44.990
And then finally, OK,
fine, I have to do RL.

01:01:44.990 --> 01:01:49.990
But some people, for whatever
reason, like doing RL.

01:01:49.990 --> 01:01:52.070
Also, what we'll,
hopefully, talk

01:01:52.070 --> 01:01:56.790
about this year is that
if you do RL at scale

01:01:56.790 --> 01:01:58.690
and try to maximize
your throughput,

01:01:58.690 --> 01:02:01.672
there's actually a lot
of systems challenges.

01:02:01.672 --> 01:02:04.130
You have to have an inference
server and a training server.

01:02:04.130 --> 01:02:07.490
And then the inference server
has to generate these rollouts.

01:02:07.490 --> 01:02:09.830
Especially if you do
RL against environments

01:02:09.830 --> 01:02:11.570
that involve code execution.

01:02:11.570 --> 01:02:13.650
It's a whole kind of
orchestration game.

01:02:13.650 --> 01:02:18.510
And then if your workers
lag behind or then

01:02:18.510 --> 01:02:22.030
you get into off policy
issues, and then you're

01:02:22.030 --> 01:02:26.670
constantly kind of juggling
this on-policyness with a desire

01:02:26.670 --> 01:02:28.710
to maximize throughput.

01:02:28.710 --> 01:02:30.870
It's a big, wonderful
mess, which, hopefully,

01:02:30.870 --> 01:02:33.790
we'll talk more about
when we come to that.

01:02:33.790 --> 01:02:39.310
So Assignment 5,
we're still deciding

01:02:39.310 --> 01:02:40.610
what exactly we want to do.

01:02:40.610 --> 01:02:42.830
Last year, it was
implemented DPO and GRPO,

01:02:42.830 --> 01:02:46.290
and get it working for
some math benchmark,

01:02:46.290 --> 01:02:50.110
but we'll see how
much farther we

01:02:50.110 --> 01:02:54.470
can push it on the realistic
dimension this year.

01:02:54.470 --> 01:02:58.910
So again, remember
it's about efficiency.

01:02:58.910 --> 01:03:02.590
And efficiency can be
either data efficiency

01:03:02.590 --> 01:03:03.752
or compute efficiency.

01:03:03.752 --> 01:03:06.210
So the way to think about it,
you have all these resources.

01:03:06.210 --> 01:03:07.910
you have data, you
have the hardware,

01:03:07.910 --> 01:03:11.510
which has compute cores, you
have memory, communication

01:03:11.510 --> 01:03:12.390
bandwidth.

01:03:12.390 --> 01:03:13.950
And you're just
trying to figure out

01:03:13.950 --> 01:03:17.550
how do you build the best model
according to some evaluation

01:03:17.550 --> 01:03:20.510
given a fixed set of resources.

01:03:20.510 --> 01:03:23.270
So through this
lens, I think, you

01:03:23.270 --> 01:03:27.110
can actually think about a
lot of these design decisions

01:03:27.110 --> 01:03:29.490
as optimizing for this.

01:03:29.490 --> 01:03:33.830
So systems, clearly, that's
about compute efficiency.

01:03:33.830 --> 01:03:38.230
Tokenization, as I mentioned
before, you can't just

01:03:38.230 --> 01:03:40.230
work with raw bytes,
but that's going

01:03:40.230 --> 01:03:44.910
to be very compute inefficient,
at least with today's model

01:03:44.910 --> 01:03:46.110
architectures.

01:03:46.110 --> 01:03:49.190
And so a lot of tokenization
is about improving

01:03:49.190 --> 01:03:50.550
compute efficiency.

01:03:50.550 --> 01:03:54.630
Model architecture, many of
the changes that we'll see

01:03:54.630 --> 01:03:58.910
are motivated by reducing
the memory or flops.

01:03:58.910 --> 01:04:01.590
In fact, a lot of them
are influenced by the need

01:04:01.590 --> 01:04:04.310
to have faster inference.

01:04:04.310 --> 01:04:06.550
Data filtering, you
can also view as--

01:04:06.550 --> 01:04:07.890
through efficiency lens.

01:04:07.890 --> 01:04:10.790
We don't want to waste
time updating gradients

01:04:10.790 --> 01:04:13.870
on a bunch of
redundant bad data,

01:04:13.870 --> 01:04:16.630
even if it might not hurt you,
but it hurts you in the sense

01:04:16.630 --> 01:04:19.510
that if you have a fixed compute
budget, more time on bad data

01:04:19.510 --> 01:04:21.270
means less time on good data.

01:04:21.270 --> 01:04:23.590
And then finally, scaling
laws is explicitly

01:04:23.590 --> 01:04:27.510
about how you can, essentially,
do effective hyperparameter

01:04:27.510 --> 01:04:30.070
tuning on much smaller models.

01:04:30.070 --> 01:04:32.690
And now tomorrow, we might
become data-constrained,

01:04:32.690 --> 01:04:37.150
and the calculus of what design
decisions you should take

01:04:37.150 --> 01:04:37.970
might change.

01:04:37.970 --> 01:04:40.230
But I think the
overall mindset which

01:04:40.230 --> 01:04:42.070
we're trying to
teach you is think

01:04:42.070 --> 01:04:46.790
about the efficiency
of your approach.

01:04:46.790 --> 01:04:49.570
OK, let me stop there.

01:04:49.570 --> 01:04:52.320
Are there any
questions about any

01:04:52.320 --> 01:04:56.300
of these assignments or topics?

01:05:05.656 --> 01:05:06.160
OK.

01:05:06.160 --> 01:05:10.380
So now let's do tokenization.

01:05:10.380 --> 01:05:15.320
So this is-- we're jumping
into our first unit here.

01:05:15.320 --> 01:05:17.400
So Andrej Karpathy has
this really good video

01:05:17.400 --> 01:05:18.340
on tokenization.

01:05:18.340 --> 01:05:21.240
You should check it out.

01:05:21.240 --> 01:05:25.980
So starting point is raw
text is what is text?

01:05:25.980 --> 01:05:28.640
It's Unicode strings.

01:05:28.640 --> 01:05:31.280
And on the other hand,
the language model

01:05:31.280 --> 01:05:33.880
places a distribution
over sequences of tokens

01:05:33.880 --> 01:05:35.880
usually represented as indices.

01:05:35.880 --> 01:05:39.040
So we need a procedure
that encodes these strings

01:05:39.040 --> 01:05:42.480
into tokens, and also a
procedure that decodes

01:05:42.480 --> 01:05:44.800
tokens back into strings.

01:05:44.800 --> 01:05:46.680
So a tokenizer is
basically something

01:05:46.680 --> 01:05:50.200
that can do this round trip.

01:05:50.200 --> 01:05:52.160
So here are some
examples to give you

01:05:52.160 --> 01:05:54.580
a flavor for how
tokenizers work.

01:05:54.580 --> 01:05:56.892
Actually, I should have tried
to get in there earlier.

01:05:56.892 --> 01:05:58.100
So this is not going to work.

01:05:58.100 --> 01:05:59.808
If you go to the site
you can play around

01:05:59.808 --> 01:06:03.280
with different tokenizers.

01:06:03.280 --> 01:06:07.320
So some observations here.

01:06:07.320 --> 01:06:12.160
And you'll appreciate why
tokenizers are kind of annoying

01:06:12.160 --> 01:06:14.080
and why people want
to get rid of them.

01:06:14.080 --> 01:06:17.200
So a word and its--

01:06:17.200 --> 01:06:21.360
a word conglomerate
with its preceding space

01:06:21.360 --> 01:06:24.320
are different tokens.

01:06:24.320 --> 01:06:27.720
So many tokens you'll
actually see are space a word,

01:06:27.720 --> 01:06:32.440
which is fine, but
kind of strange.

01:06:32.440 --> 01:06:36.120
So this hello and
this hello is actually

01:06:36.120 --> 01:06:38.840
two completely different
indices that have nothing

01:06:38.840 --> 01:06:40.560
to do with each other.

01:06:40.560 --> 01:06:45.220
And sometimes, depending
on the tokenizer you use,

01:06:45.220 --> 01:06:48.920
numbers are represented with--

01:06:48.920 --> 01:06:50.540
every few digits is a token.

01:06:50.540 --> 01:06:52.940
Sometimes, it's predictable,
and sometimes, it's not.

01:06:52.940 --> 01:06:55.045
Some tokenizers try to
make every digit a token,

01:06:55.045 --> 01:06:57.420
but then you're blowing up
the number of tokens you have.

01:06:57.420 --> 01:07:00.240
So there's some trade off there.

01:07:00.240 --> 01:07:04.960
So here's the GPT-5 tokenizer.

01:07:04.960 --> 01:07:14.720
So you can take this string and
convert it into these indices.

01:07:14.720 --> 01:07:18.120
And then you can decode
it back into the string.

01:07:18.120 --> 01:07:20.357
And so tokenizers
should round trip.

01:07:20.357 --> 01:07:22.440
If you implement tokenizer,
it doesn't round trip,

01:07:22.440 --> 01:07:25.040
you have a problem.

01:07:25.040 --> 01:07:32.360
So the compression ratio here is
the number of bytes per token.

01:07:32.360 --> 01:07:37.760
So in this case, we have, the
number of bytes of this string

01:07:37.760 --> 01:07:38.640
is 20.

01:07:38.640 --> 01:07:40.480
The number of tokens is 8.

01:07:40.480 --> 01:07:41.980
And you do 20 divided by 8.

01:07:41.980 --> 01:07:44.240
So the compression ratio is 2.5.

01:07:44.240 --> 01:07:46.640
OK, so 2.5 bytes per token.

01:07:46.640 --> 01:07:48.130
The larger the
compression ratio,

01:07:48.130 --> 01:07:49.880
that means the shorter
the sentence, which

01:07:49.880 --> 01:07:51.820
is good because
attention is quadratic,

01:07:51.820 --> 01:07:56.200
and you want to make sure
the sent sequence is shorter.

01:07:56.200 --> 01:07:58.560
Now, you could obviously
increase the compression ratio

01:07:58.560 --> 01:08:01.760
by increasing the vocab
size, but then, you

01:08:01.760 --> 01:08:05.040
get into sparsity
where more and more--

01:08:05.040 --> 01:08:07.120
because every element
of vocab is treated

01:08:07.120 --> 01:08:10.120
like a distinct element.

01:08:10.120 --> 01:08:13.760
So these days, tokenizers,
especially multilingual

01:08:13.760 --> 01:08:22.359
tokenizers, have 100k or
200k tokens, distinct tokens.

01:08:22.359 --> 01:08:25.260
So you can look at the
GPD token vocabulary.

01:08:25.260 --> 01:08:28.760
I think we'll skip it
in the interest of time.

01:08:28.760 --> 01:08:30.700
So how do you build a tokenizer?

01:08:30.700 --> 01:08:34.143
So I'm going to go
through this fast.

01:08:34.143 --> 01:08:35.560
So the first thing
you might do is

01:08:35.560 --> 01:08:37.960
like, well, Unicode
string, that's

01:08:37.960 --> 01:08:40.380
a sequence of
Unicode characters.

01:08:40.380 --> 01:08:43.340
And each character
is an integer,

01:08:43.340 --> 01:08:45.540
which you can call
ord in Python,

01:08:45.540 --> 01:08:47.439
and you get some number out.

01:08:47.439 --> 01:08:49.500
And this can be converted
into characters.

01:08:49.500 --> 01:08:52.840
So let's just build a
character level tokenizer which

01:08:52.840 --> 01:08:55.120
basically breaks
up each character

01:08:55.120 --> 01:08:57.319
and encodes it in a token.

01:08:57.319 --> 01:08:59.720
And then this can decode back.

01:08:59.720 --> 01:09:01.080
Life is good.

01:09:01.080 --> 01:09:05.060
So now, there are 150k
Unicode characters.

01:09:05.060 --> 01:09:12.140
So your vocab size could
be 150k, which is a lot.

01:09:12.140 --> 01:09:15.399
I mean, it's not
crazy, but I think

01:09:15.399 --> 01:09:19.319
the bigger problem is that many
characters are actually rare,

01:09:19.319 --> 01:09:23.760
which means that it's really an
inefficient use of a vocabulary.

01:09:23.760 --> 01:09:27.960
And also, the compression
ratio, which reflects this

01:09:27.960 --> 01:09:29.500
is not that great.

01:09:29.500 --> 01:09:33.840
So most of the time, you're
actually using a lot of tokens

01:09:33.840 --> 01:09:35.620
to represent your sequence.

01:09:35.620 --> 01:09:41.790
And many of the indices are
actually not being very used.

01:09:41.790 --> 01:09:45.170
So this is not a
very good tokenizer.

01:09:45.170 --> 01:09:47.770
So here's another attempt.

01:09:47.770 --> 01:09:50.370
So you can turn
strings into bytes.

01:09:50.370 --> 01:09:57.770
So Unicode has a UTF-8 encoding,
which means that you can--

01:09:57.770 --> 01:10:02.970
sometimes a string like "a"
is just 1 byte, and sometimes,

01:10:02.970 --> 01:10:05.930
a string is multiple bytes.

01:10:05.930 --> 01:10:08.570
So let's build a
tokenizer around that.

01:10:08.570 --> 01:10:12.290
So we can take this
string and convert it

01:10:12.290 --> 01:10:14.890
into a sequence of bytes.

01:10:14.890 --> 01:10:17.730
And notice that this is
a longer sequence now,

01:10:17.730 --> 01:10:23.990
but all the numbers
are between 0 and 255,

01:10:23.990 --> 01:10:26.370
because that's
what a byte means.

01:10:26.370 --> 01:10:34.250
And the compression ratio
is 1, which is not great.

01:10:34.250 --> 01:10:38.490
So byte sequences
can be very long,

01:10:38.490 --> 01:10:40.830
but the vocab size is small.

01:10:44.630 --> 01:10:45.130
OK.

01:10:45.130 --> 01:10:48.250
So both of these are really bad.

01:10:48.250 --> 01:10:50.510
So let's try to
make some progress.

01:10:50.510 --> 01:10:53.650
So this is what actually
people used to do in NLP,

01:10:53.650 --> 01:10:55.510
if people remember.

01:10:55.510 --> 01:10:59.130
So if you take a string, I
can just chunk it up into--

01:10:59.130 --> 01:11:04.050
break it up by spaces or
some regular expression.

01:11:04.050 --> 01:11:09.610
And let's just call each
of these chunks a token.

01:11:09.610 --> 01:11:14.570
So what is good about this one
is that each token is meaningful

01:11:14.570 --> 01:11:16.370
because humans invented
words, and words

01:11:16.370 --> 01:11:19.890
tend to have a stable
semantic meaning.

01:11:19.890 --> 01:11:21.513
But your vocab
size is the number

01:11:21.513 --> 01:11:22.930
of distinct chunks
in the training

01:11:22.930 --> 01:11:25.010
data, which could be a lot.

01:11:25.010 --> 01:11:30.630
And also, your compression
ratio, I mean, it's quite good,

01:11:30.630 --> 01:11:32.790
but the vocabulary can be huge.

01:11:32.790 --> 01:11:38.410
Actually, it's worse than that
because though the vocabulary

01:11:38.410 --> 01:11:40.970
could be actually
unbounded, right?

01:11:40.970 --> 01:11:43.290
Because at test time, you
might get some sequence

01:11:43.290 --> 01:11:45.850
and you tokenize, and
then you have a token

01:11:45.850 --> 01:11:47.170
you've never seen before.

01:11:47.170 --> 01:11:50.430
And people used to
assign these UNK token,

01:11:50.430 --> 01:11:52.250
but that's really
ugly and can mess up

01:11:52.250 --> 01:11:55.130
your perplexity calculations.

01:11:55.130 --> 01:11:58.450
So this is also not great.

01:11:58.450 --> 01:12:00.730
OK, so what we're
actually going to do

01:12:00.730 --> 01:12:03.530
is called byte pair encoding.

01:12:03.530 --> 01:12:06.130
And this was introduced a long
time ago for data compression.

01:12:06.130 --> 01:12:12.890
Way before language models were
really on the scene, really.

01:12:12.890 --> 01:12:16.370
It was first introduced to
NLP for doing neural machine

01:12:16.370 --> 01:12:18.130
translation.

01:12:18.130 --> 01:12:27.810
And the first paper that
used BPE for LLMs was GPT-2.

01:12:27.810 --> 01:12:29.570
So the basic idea
is that you're going

01:12:29.570 --> 01:12:32.570
to train the
tokenizer on raw text

01:12:32.570 --> 01:12:36.072
to construct a vocabulary
that's tailored to the data.

01:12:36.072 --> 01:12:38.530
And you're also going to have
this property that everything

01:12:38.530 --> 01:12:39.910
becomes-- can be tokenized.

01:12:39.910 --> 01:12:42.570
If it's rare, then it just
breaks up into smaller units

01:12:42.570 --> 01:12:46.290
rather than having
this UNK token.

01:12:46.290 --> 01:12:51.733
So common sequences are going
to be represented as one token.

01:12:51.733 --> 01:12:54.150
Rare sequences are going to
be split into multiple tokens.

01:12:54.150 --> 01:12:55.490
That's the idea.

01:12:55.490 --> 01:12:59.570
So the algorithm is fairly
simple, conceptually.

01:12:59.570 --> 01:13:02.830
So you start, basically,
with your corpus.

01:13:02.830 --> 01:13:05.170
Let's assume it's
one long sequence.

01:13:05.170 --> 01:13:06.810
You get a byte sequence.

01:13:06.810 --> 01:13:08.510
Each byte starts as a token.

01:13:08.510 --> 01:13:12.690
And then we're going to
merge successive pairs

01:13:12.690 --> 01:13:16.570
of adjacent tokens that
occur the most frequently.

01:13:16.570 --> 01:13:20.090
So let's step through how
this is going to work in code.

01:13:20.090 --> 01:13:22.970
So here's a simple string,
"the cat in the hat,"

01:13:22.970 --> 01:13:28.010
and here's the implementation
of the BPE algorithm.

01:13:28.010 --> 01:13:33.140
So we're going to turn that
into a sequence of bytes.

01:13:33.140 --> 01:13:37.340
And then we're going to--

01:13:37.340 --> 01:13:39.700
first, we're going
to, basically, count

01:13:39.700 --> 01:13:42.320
the number of times
successive tokens appear.

01:13:42.320 --> 01:13:48.380
So 116, 104 shows up
twice, so we get this.

01:13:48.380 --> 01:13:54.860
And then we're going to
find the pair that happens

01:13:54.860 --> 01:13:56.600
the most number of times.

01:13:56.600 --> 01:13:57.752
So that's 116, 104.

01:13:57.752 --> 01:14:01.460
I guess there's a few ties, but
we'll just take the first one.

01:14:01.460 --> 01:14:03.540
And then we're going
to merge that pair.

01:14:03.540 --> 01:14:08.580
And by merging that pair what
we do is we create a new token.

01:14:08.580 --> 01:14:13.640
In this case, this is going
to be called token 256.

01:14:13.640 --> 01:14:16.620
It's going to
represent this pair,

01:14:16.620 --> 01:14:19.420
and we're going to add
it to our vocabulary.

01:14:19.420 --> 01:14:23.700
So 256 is going to represent
the sequence of th.

01:14:23.700 --> 01:14:25.360
So t and h have been merged.

01:14:25.360 --> 01:14:27.820
And we're going to call--
every time we see th,

01:14:27.820 --> 01:14:31.540
we're going to use
256 to represent that.

01:14:31.540 --> 01:14:33.740
And then we go through
indices, and then we

01:14:33.740 --> 01:14:37.860
replace every occurrence
of 116, 104 with 256.

01:14:37.860 --> 01:14:40.340
So those two places
have been replaced.

01:14:40.340 --> 01:14:42.540
And then we iterate.

01:14:42.540 --> 01:14:48.620
So the next time we do this,
we're going to find 256 and 101.

01:14:48.620 --> 01:14:50.340
We're going to merge that.

01:14:50.340 --> 01:14:52.580
And now we have 257.

01:14:52.580 --> 01:14:56.460
And then we're going to
merge that one more time,

01:14:56.460 --> 01:14:58.360
and we're going to get 258.

01:14:58.360 --> 01:15:02.780
So over time, the
sequence is shrinking

01:15:02.780 --> 01:15:06.780
and the vocabulary
size is growing.

01:15:06.780 --> 01:15:07.720
OK.

01:15:07.720 --> 01:15:16.980
So let me-- and the
compression ratio here is--

01:15:16.980 --> 01:15:21.460
that we get for this, I
mean, this toy example is 1.5

01:15:21.460 --> 01:15:21.960
OK.

01:15:21.960 --> 01:15:25.340
So now that you
have a tokenizer,

01:15:25.340 --> 01:15:27.460
how do you tokenize new text?

01:15:27.460 --> 01:15:31.780
Well, you take a new
string and you encode it.

01:15:31.780 --> 01:15:35.540
And conceptually, what
happens is that you basically

01:15:35.540 --> 01:15:39.780
go through the set of
merges that you've made,

01:15:39.780 --> 01:15:44.440
and then you just apply
the merges to your string.

01:15:44.440 --> 01:15:48.340
OK, let me actually not
step through that code.

01:15:48.340 --> 01:15:52.100
So that will give
you a sequence.

01:15:52.100 --> 01:15:56.100
So this is a sequence encoding
of "the quick brown fox."

01:15:56.100 --> 01:16:00.860
And then when you decode it,
you get the same thing back.

01:16:00.860 --> 01:16:04.000
So I went through
this a bit fast,

01:16:04.000 --> 01:16:06.800
just in the interest of time.

01:16:06.800 --> 01:16:11.040
I will say that this
implementation works.

01:16:11.040 --> 01:16:13.380
This is a full-blown
BPE implementation.

01:16:13.380 --> 01:16:15.900
It's extremely slow.

01:16:15.900 --> 01:16:20.140
So in Assignment 1, we're
going to ask you to,

01:16:20.140 --> 01:16:22.980
basically, make it faster.

01:16:22.980 --> 01:16:28.380
So currently, encode
loops over all the merges,

01:16:28.380 --> 01:16:30.500
which is very slow
because you might have--

01:16:30.500 --> 01:16:33.780
the number of merges
you have is essentially

01:16:33.780 --> 01:16:37.260
the vocab size minus 256.

01:16:37.260 --> 01:16:41.540
So you only want to loop
over the merges that matter.

01:16:41.540 --> 01:16:44.860
And you have to build some
indices to make that happen.

01:16:44.860 --> 01:16:48.340
There's some details
around special tokens.

01:16:48.340 --> 01:16:50.380
Conceptually not deep,
but important to building

01:16:50.380 --> 01:16:52.460
a modern tokenizer.

01:16:52.460 --> 01:16:55.420
Another thing is that I've
presented the tokenizer just

01:16:55.420 --> 01:16:56.280
for simplicity.

01:16:56.280 --> 01:17:00.260
As you take an entire string
and then you try to tokenize it,

01:17:00.260 --> 01:17:04.300
really, what happens is
that you break it up into--

01:17:04.300 --> 01:17:06.180
your text into
chunks, and then you

01:17:06.180 --> 01:17:08.660
apply tokenizer on each chunk.

01:17:08.660 --> 01:17:10.760
So that's going
to be much faster.

01:17:13.740 --> 01:17:16.620
And then try to make
it as fast as possible.

01:17:16.620 --> 01:17:19.540
At some point you might
realize that Python is just not

01:17:19.540 --> 01:17:20.600
very fast.

01:17:20.600 --> 01:17:25.550
And if you want to implement it
in your favorite language, Rust

01:17:25.550 --> 01:17:29.190
or C or something,
then go for it.

01:17:29.190 --> 01:17:31.370
OK, so quick summary.

01:17:31.370 --> 01:17:37.430
Tokenizers convert between
strings and tokens or indices.

01:17:37.430 --> 01:17:39.310
The previous
character-base, byte-base,

01:17:39.310 --> 01:17:44.190
word-base are highly
suboptimal in their own way.

01:17:44.190 --> 01:17:48.850
BPE is effective heuristic
that is data driven.

01:17:48.850 --> 01:17:53.430
So it seems to be
pretty effective.

01:17:53.430 --> 01:17:56.430
Now, like I said before,
maybe next year, I

01:17:56.430 --> 01:17:58.590
don't have to teach
this, but for this year,

01:17:58.590 --> 01:18:01.950
we're stuck with tokenization.

01:18:01.950 --> 01:18:05.270
Even if we get rid of
tokenization though,

01:18:05.270 --> 01:18:08.350
I think whatever solution
replaces it, I think,

01:18:08.350 --> 01:18:11.350
has to satisfy the
following properties.

01:18:11.350 --> 01:18:14.790
If you have the
model, the transformer

01:18:14.790 --> 01:18:18.970
needs to operate on some sort
of abstractions of the sequence.

01:18:18.970 --> 01:18:22.470
And this is most evident if
you think about not just text,

01:18:22.470 --> 01:18:30.390
but video or DNA sequences where
the individual bytes or units

01:18:30.390 --> 01:18:35.550
are actually quite
low signal to noise,

01:18:35.550 --> 01:18:38.110
and you have to do some
sort of abstraction

01:18:38.110 --> 01:18:41.750
to lift it into a place where
you can do modeling on that.

01:18:41.750 --> 01:18:44.350
And then finally,
as I mentioned,

01:18:44.350 --> 01:18:45.930
chunks should be variable.

01:18:45.930 --> 01:18:48.070
You want adaptive computation.

01:18:48.070 --> 01:18:50.613
Not all bytes are
treated the same.

01:18:50.613 --> 01:18:52.030
And if you don't
do that, I think,

01:18:52.030 --> 01:18:53.710
you're going to be suboptimal.

01:18:53.710 --> 01:18:56.110
So any end-to-end
solution also, I think,

01:18:56.110 --> 01:18:58.790
has to have these properties.

01:18:58.790 --> 01:18:59.330
OK.

01:18:59.330 --> 01:19:01.430
So with that, I will end.

01:19:01.430 --> 01:19:03.550
Next time, on
Wednesday, we're going

01:19:03.550 --> 01:19:06.490
to start the unit on
resource accounting.

01:19:06.490 --> 01:19:11.070
Which is sort of a baby
system, I would say.

01:19:11.070 --> 01:19:13.910
And then after that, we're going
to go back into architectures

01:19:13.910 --> 01:19:15.470
and go from there.

01:19:15.470 --> 01:19:17.220
All right.

